hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"4841","And I remember from him: I once passed in front of him in a heap of charity dates, took a date and put it in my mouth, so he seized me by the nape, then put his hand in my mouth and pulled it out, spitting it out, then threw it into the heap. His companions said: If only you had left the boy to eat it. He said: Charity is not lawful for the family of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4842","He said: And he taught me words to supplicate with at the end of the qunut: O Allah, guide me among those You have guided, grant me well-being among those You have granted well-being, befriend me among those You have befriended, bless for me what You have given, and shield me from the evil of what You have decreed. Indeed You decree and none decrees upon You, and indeed whoever You befriend is not humiliated. Blessed are You, our Lord, and exalted. Abu al-Hawra' said: I visited Muhammad ibn 'Ali while he was under siege, and I narrated it to him from al-Hasan. Muhammad said: They are words we were taught to supplicate with in the qunut. Then he mentioned this supplication like the hadith of al-Hasan ibn 'Umara."
"4843","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Maryam, from al-Hasan ibn 'Ali, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught him to say in the qunut."
"4844","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, that 'Umar performed qunut after bowing, and that 'Uthman performed qunut before bowing so that the people could catch the rak'a."
"4845","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan, regarding a man who missed one rak'a of the morning prayer, prayed one rak'a with the imam, and performed qunut with him. He said: When he makes up the last rak'a, he performs qunut again. Ma'mar said: And Qatada said: He does not perform qunut. Ma'mar said: If he performs qunut, it is good, and if he does not perform qunut, there is no harm."
"4846","From al-Thawri, from al-Nu'man ibn Qays, who said: I prayed behind 'Abayda, and he performed qunut in Fajr before bowing."
"4847","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Others say in the qunut: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate: O Allah, to You we pray and to You we prostrate, You alone we worship; to You we pray and prostrate, to You we hasten and hurry; we hope for Your mercy and fear Your severe punishment; indeed Your punishment overtakes the disbelievers. O Allah, we seek Your help, seek Your forgiveness, praise You and do not disbelieve in You; we believe in You, renounce and abandon whoever disobeys You. O Allah, we have submitted ourselves to You, turned our faces to You, entrusted our backs to You, out of desire and fear of You; there is no refuge nor escape from You except to You; I believe in Your Book which You sent down and Your Messenger whom You sent. O Allah, forgive the believing men and women, the Muslim men and women, set right their relations, join their hearts, place faith and wisdom in their hearts, inspire them to fulfill Your covenant which You made with them, take them upon the creed of Your Prophet, grant them victory over Your enemy and theirs, O God of truth. O Allah, punish the disbelievers, cast terror into their hearts, divide their unity, send down upon them Your wrath and punishment. O Allah, punish the disbelievers among the People of the Book who belie Your messengers and bar from Your path. O Allah, forgive us, have mercy on us, and be pleased with us."
"4848","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Ubayy ibn Ka'b performed qunut in witr after bowing."
"4849","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from al-Nakha'i, that Ibn Mas'ud would perform qunut the whole year in witr."
"4850","From al-Thawri, from Aban, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqama, from 'Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he performed qunut in witr before bowing."
"4851","From al-Thawri, from al-Ash'ath, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, who said: The qunut in witr throughout the whole year is before bowing."
"4852","From Hisham, that al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin would perform qunut in witr before bowing. And 'Abd al-Razzaq said: He says takbir when he raises his head from bowing, then says takbir again when he goes down; and we adopt this."
"4853","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: There is no qunut throughout the whole year except in the last half of Ramadan. Ma'mar said: I perform qunut the whole year except the first half of Ramadan, for I do not perform qunut in it. And al-Hasan used to do likewise, and Qatada and others mentioned it from him."
"4854","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, that he would perform qunut the whole year in witr except the first half of Ramadan. He said: And Ibn Sirin would not perform qunut in anything of the year except the last half of Ramadan."
"4855","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr ibn 'Adi, from Ibrahim: He deemed it preferable to say in the qunut of witr with these two surahs: O Allah, we seek Your help, seek Your forgiveness, praise You and do not disbelieve in You; we renounce and abandon whoever disobeys You. O Allah, You alone we worship, to You we pray and prostrate, to You we hasten and hurry; we hope for Your mercy and fear Your punishment; indeed Your punishment overtakes the disbelievers."
"4856","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Hands were not raised in witr in Ramadan."
"4857","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab said: Hands were not raised in witr in Ramadan."
"4858","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to 'Ata': The supplication of the people of Mecca after they finish witr in the month of Ramadan? He said: An innovation. He said: I found the people, and that was not done in Mecca, until I narrate a hadith."
"4859","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, who said: He says takbir when he finishes the recitation of the last rak'a of witr, then performs qunut raising his voice; then when he wants to bow, he says takbir again. Al-Mughira said, from Ibrahim: And he raises his hands in witr. He said: The standing in qunut is the length of: When the sky splits. And from him also, from Ibrahim, who said: I visited al-Aswad while he was ill, so I stood standing with a man supporting him, and he lengthened it for fear of falling short of what he used to perform qunut - from Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Mughira, from Simak, from Ibrahim."
"4860","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would raise his hands level with his chest when he supplicated, then wipe his face with them. He said: And I saw Ma'mar do it. We said to 'Abd al-Razzaq: Do you raise your hands when you supplicate in witr? He said: Yes, a little at its end."
"4861","From Dawud ibn Qays, from Isma'il ibn Rafi', from Ja'far ibn Abi Talib, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Shall I not give you a gift? Shall I not grant you? Shall I not present to you? Shall I not favor you? Shall I not? Shall I not? - until I thought he would allot me the water of al-Bahrayn. He said: You pray four rak'as, reciting the Mother of the Qur'an in each rak'a along with a surah, then you say: Praise be to Allah, Glory be to Allah, Allah is Greatest, and there is no god but Allah - count it as one until you count fifteen times. Then you bow and say it ten times while bowing, then you rise and say it ten times while rising, then you prostrate and say it ten times while prostrating, then you rise and say it ten times while sitting, then you prostrate and say it ten times while prostrating, then you rise and say it ten times while sitting - that is seventy-five; and in the last three likewise, making three hundred in total; and if you separate them, they are one thousand two hundred. And he deemed it preferable to recite the surah after the Mother of the Qur'an as twenty verses or more. Do them in your day or night, or your week, or in a month, or in a year, or in your lifetime; for even if your sins were as many as the stars of the sky, the drops of rain, the grains of sand of 'Alij, or the days of time, Allah would forgive them for you."
"4862","Ma'mar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu Malih ibn Usama, from Burayda, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever deliberately abandons the 'Asr prayer, Allah annuls his deeds."
"4863","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, that Jabir ibn 'Abdullah said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is nothing between one of you and disbelief except abandoning a prescribed prayer."
"4864","From 'Umar ibn Zayd, who said: I heard Abu al-Zubayr narrate that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is between the servant and polytheism is only abandoning prayer."
"4865","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, that he heard Makhul say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever deliberately abandons prayer, the protection of Allah is absolved from him. Abu Bakr said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash informed me, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Ubayd al-Kala'i, that Makhul informed him likewise from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he said to him: O Abu Wahb, whoever is absolved of Allah's protection has disbelieved."
"4866","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Abandoning prayer is polytheism."
"4867","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: I heard 'Umar say: He who abandons prayer has no share in Islam."
"4868","From Ma'mar, from al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Sila ibn Zufar, from Hudhayfa, who said: Islam is built upon eight shares: testifying that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, establishing prayer, giving zakat, pilgrimage to the House, fasting the month of Ramadan, jihad, enjoining good and forbidding evil; and he who has no share has failed."
"4869","From Ibn al-Taymi, who said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr narrated to me, saying: al-Hawari ibn Ziyad narrated to me, saying: I was sitting with Ibn 'Umar when a young man came to him and said: Will you not strive in jihad? He fell silent, then turned away from him, then he repeated and he fell silent and turned away from him, then he asked him, and Ibn 'Umar said: Islam is built upon four pillars: establishing prayer, giving zakat - do not separate the two - fasting Ramadan, and pilgrimage to the House for whoever can find a way to it; and jihad and charity are among good deeds."
"4870","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Abu Bakr and 'Umar said to a man: Pray the prayer Allah has obligated upon you at its time, for in neglecting it lies ruin."
"4871","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu 'Ubayda, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Which is best? He said: Deeds are: the prayers at their times, dutifulness to parents, and jihad in the path of Allah."
"4872","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', who said: A woman calls the iqama for herself when she wants to pray."
"4873","Ibn Jurayj said: Tawus said: 'A'isha would call the adhan and the iqama."
"4874","From Ibn al-Taymi, and Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Layth, from Tawus, who said: 'A'isha would call the adhan and the iqama."
"4875","From al-Thawri, from 'Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from Mujahid, who said: There is no iqama upon women."
"4876","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', who said: If there is a man with the women, they are not prevented from calling the adhan and the iqama then."
"4877","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: There is no iqama upon women."
"4878","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan and Ibn al-Musayyab, who said: There is no adhan nor iqama upon women."
"4879","Al-Thawri, from a man, from Ibrahim, who said: There is no adhan nor iqama upon women. And 'Uthman ibn Matar mentioned it from Sa'id, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim."
"4880","From 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, who said: There is no adhan nor iqama upon women."
"4881","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, who said: There is no iqama upon women."
"4882","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from 'Ikrima, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: There is no adhan nor iqama upon women. Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man, from 'Ikrima, likewise."
"4883","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, who said: A woman prays in a shirt and a headscarf."
"4884","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Umm al-Hasan, who said: I saw Umm Salama, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), praying in a shirt and a headscarf."
"4885","From Malik, from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, from his mother, that she asked Umm Salama: In how many garments does a woman pray? She said: In the headscarf and the ample shirt that conceals the tops of her feet."
"4886","From al-Awza'i, from Makhul, from one who asked 'A'isha: In how many garments does a woman pray? She said to him: Ask 'Ali, then return to me and tell me what he says to you. He said: So he went to 'Ali and asked him, and he said: In the headscarf and the ample shirt. He returned to 'A'isha and told her, and she said: He spoke the truth."
"4887","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Umm Thawr, from her husband Bishr, who said: I said to Ibn 'Abbas: In how many garments does a woman pray? He said: In a shirt and a headscarf."
"4888","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Layla bint Sa'd informed me that she saw 'A'isha, the Mother of the Believers, praying in the house wearing a waist-wrapper, with a thick shirt and headscarf, and nothing else upon her."
"4889","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hukayma informed me, from Umayya, that Umm Habiba, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), prayed in a shirt and a waist-wrapper which she wrapped as a head-cover until it touched the ground, without wrapping it as a waist-wrapper, and she had no headscarf upon her."
"4890","From Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from 'Ikrima, who said: If a woman takes a garment and covers her head with it so that none of her hair is seen, it suffices her in place of the headscarf."
"4891","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, who said: 'Ikrima was asked: Does a woman pray in a shirt and headscarf? He said: Yes, if it is not transparent."
"4892","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn 'Urwa, from his father, who said: Her shirt suffices her if it is ample - I only know he said: along with the headscarf."
"4893","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', who said: A woman prays in her shirt, her headscarf, and her waist-wrapper; and that she wear the outer cloak is dearer to me. I said: What if her shirt and headscarf are thin, one of them? He said: Then the outer cloak over that, because of the angels, for they are with her. I said: Her shirt reaches the knees? He said: No, until it is ample and thick. He said: Let her wrap the waist-wrapper and tie it over her waist."
"4894","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Do not be lax in concealing the waist, for if what is below the waist is hidden, it is more covering; and if there is anything in it, it conceals it better."
"4895","From Ma'mar, from 'Amr, from al-Hasan, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any girl who reaches puberty and does not wear a headscarf, Allah will not accept prayer from her."
"4896","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: A woman does not go out except girded. And 'Amr ibn Dinar said: That is what used to be said."
"4897","From al-Thawri, from Khusayf, from Mujahid, who said: If a free woman who has reached puberty prays without a headscarf, Allah will not accept prayer from her."
"4898","From al-Thawri, who said: I was informed from Ibrahim, who said: When a woman reaches puberty, wearing the headscarf is obligatory upon her, as upon her mother."
"4899","Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, who said: It is said that when a woman reaches puberty, no prayer is accepted from her until she wears a headscarf and covers her head."
"4900","Muhammad ibn Muslim, from al-Sabbah, from Mujahid, who said: It reached me that a woman fell from her mount, her clothes were lifted from her, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was near her, so he turned away from her. It was said: She is wearing trousers. He said: May Allah have mercy on women who wear trousers."
"4901","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: He disliked a woman praying with no necklace on her neck. I said: Why? He said: So that she does not resemble men."
"4902","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from a man called Ibrahim, who said: Umm al-Fadl bint Ghaylan, the daughter of Yazid ibn al-Muhallab, wrote to Anas ibn Malik: Does a woman pray with no necklace on her neck? He said: So he wrote to her: A woman does not pray except with a necklace on her neck - even if she finds nothing but a strap."
"4903","From Ibn 'Uyayna, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Hasan ibn Muhammad, who said: There was a woman in Medina called Sharr, whose name was Damlamaka, and 'Umar ordered her to lay aside the outer cloak."
"4904","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to 'Ata': A young girl who has not menstruated and she prays? He said: Her waist-wrapper suffices her."
"4905","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: More than one of the people of Medina informed me that he said: Upon one who has not menstruated there is neither head-cover nor outer cloak."
"4906","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il al-Hanafi, from Abu Zayd, from 'A'isha, who said: The headscarf is only what covers the hair and the skin."
"4907","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Wahb, the freedman of Abu Ahmad, from Umm Salama, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon her while she was wrapping her headscarf, and said: One wrap, not two."
"4908","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Ibn Sirin: He disliked a woman praying with her ear outside the headscarf."
"4909","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to 'Ata': What is the minimum of garments that suffices a slave-woman? He said: We say concerning her what 'Umar said: She has cast her outer garment behind the house, so her waist-wrapper and shirt suffice her. He said: And she puts part of her shirt over her head. I said: What if she is married to a slave? He said: Likewise a slave-woman with a slave. I said: What if she is married to a freeman? He said: Then let her wrap herself fully, praying in her waist-wrapper, shirt, and headscarf."
"4910","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan: He would order a slave-woman, when she married a slave or a freeman, to wear a headscarf. He said: And al-Hasan did not consider a headscarf obligatory upon a slave-woman, unless she married or her master had relations with her."
"4911","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to 'Ata': Does a woman pray in a long gown? He said: Yes; I was informed that slave-women in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and after him would not pray until one of them placed her waist-wrapper over her head as a head-cover, or a headscarf, or a rag in which her head was concealed."
"4912","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', who said: Slave-women, when they prayed, would cast a rag over their heads; thus they would do in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 'Abd al-Razzaq said: And I heard him narrate it from Ibn Jurayj."
"4913","From al-Thawri, and Ibn 'Uyayna, from al-Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, who said: A slave-woman prays without a headscarf, praying as she goes out."
"4914","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to 'Ata': Does a slave-woman who has menstruated pray without a headscarf? He said: Yes."
"4915","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: It reached me from elders among the people of Medina that head-covers are upon slave-women when they menstruate, but outer cloaks are not upon them."
"4916","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Ata' informed me that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab would forbid slave-women the outer cloaks, lest they resemble free women."
"4917","Ibn Jurayj said: And it was narrated to me that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab struck 'Aqila, the slave-woman of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, regarding the outer cloak, for wearing one."
"4918","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to 'Ata': Does a woman wear the outer cloak with no headscarf upon her? He said: It does no harm."
"4919","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that 'Umar saw a young slave-girl who had come out of Hafsa's house adorned, wearing an outer cloak - or from the house of one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 'Umar entered the house and said: Who is this girl? They said: A slave-woman of ours - or they said: a slave-woman of such-and-such family. He became angry with them and said: Do you bring out your slave-women in their adornment, tempting people?"
"4920","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi', that Safiyya bint Abi 'Ubayd narrated to him that 'Umar, while delivering the sermon to the people, saw a slave-woman who had come out of Hafsa's house, moving through the people dressed in the garments of free women. When he finished, he visited Hafsa bint 'Umar and said: Who is the woman who came out from your house moving among the men? She said: That is a girl, the girl of 'Abd al-Rahman. He said: What makes you dress your brother's girl in the garments of free women? I visited you and took her for none but a free woman, and I intended to punish her."
"4921","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', who said: If a slave-woman prays, she conceals her head with her headscarf or a rag; thus they would do in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and after him. And likewise I saw it in the book of al-Thawri."
"4922","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from Anas, that 'Umar struck a slave-woman of the family of Anas whom he saw veiled, saying: Uncover your head; do not resemble free women."
"4923","From Ibn 'Uyayna, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Hasan ibn Muhammad, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab would forbid slave-women from wearing the outer cloaks."
"4924","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to 'Ata': Does a woman gesture with her hands like men with the takbir? He said: She does not raise her hands with that like men. And he gestured, lowering his hands greatly and drawing them to himself. And he said: A woman has a posture that a man does not have."
"4925","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', who said: A woman draws her hands together in her standing as much as she can."
"4926","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatada, who said: When a woman prostrates, she draws herself together as much as she can, and does not spread out, so that she does not raise her buttocks."
"4927","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', who said: A woman gathers herself when she bows, raising her hands to her belly, gathering herself as much as she can; and when she prostrates, let her draw her hands to herself, draw her belly and chest to her thighs, and gather herself as much as she can."
"4928","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a woman praying with something on her head."
"4929","From Ma'mar, and al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: A woman was commanded to place her forearm and belly upon her thighs when she prostrates, and not to spread out as a man spreads out, so that she does not raise her buttocks."
"4930","From Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from 'Ali, who said: When a woman prostrates, let her crouch and press her thighs to her belly."
"4931","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When she raises her head from prostration in other than a two-rak'a sitting, she does not squat, but she sits as she sits in a two-rak'a sitting."
"4932","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Safiyya bint Abi Ubayd, when she sat in a two- or four-rak'a sitting, would sit cross-legged."
"4933","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: The sitting of the woman between the two prostrations is leaning on her left side, and her sitting for the tashahhud is cross-legged."
"4934","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The sitting of the woman between the two prostrations is like her sitting in a two-rak'a sitting."
"4935","From al-Thawri and Mamar, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: The woman is commanded in prayer, in a two-rak'a sitting, to join her thighs together to one side."
"4936","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The woman sits in a two-rak'a sitting however she wishes, when she draws herself together."
"4937","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: The woman sits in a two-rak'a sitting however is easy for her."
"4938","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: The woman leads women in prayer without going out in front of them, but she stands level with them in the obligatory and voluntary prayer. I said: Even if they are many so that they become two rows or more? He said: And she should stand in their middle."
"4939","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, and Ata, they said: The woman leads women in the obligatory and voluntary prayer, standing in their middle."
"4940","From al-Thawri, from Ammar al-Duhni, from Hujayra bint Husayn, she said: Umm Salama led us in the Asr prayer, standing among us."
"4941","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The woman leads women, standing in their midst."
"4942","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim and al-Shabi, they said: There is no harm in a woman leading women in prayer in Ramadan, standing in their midst."
"4943","From Mamar, he said: The woman leads women in Ramadan and stands with them in the row. Mamar said: And one who heard Ikrima informed me, he said the like of that."
"4944","From al-Thawri, from Maysara ibn Habib al-Nahdi, from Rita al-Hanafiyya, that Aisha led them and stood among them in an obligatory prayer."
"4945","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me that Aisha used to lead women in voluntary prayer, standing with them in the row."
"4946","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb said: When a man cannot recite anything of the Quran, then he leads, and the woman stands behind him, and she prays with his prayer."
"4947","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: When a man who cannot recite is with women, he goes forward and a woman recites from behind him; when he says takbir she bows with his bowing and prostrates with his prostration."
"4948","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about hair joined to the head, and the pad placed on the head for hair: if a woman wishes she puts it on her head? He said: As for joining, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the woman who joins hair and the one who has it joined. Anas said at that time: And the eater of usury, and its giver, and the witness, and the scribe, and the tattooing woman, and the one who is tattooed, and the slanderer, and the one who seeks slander. Ata said: We have heard that. He said: And the women of the Arabs used to tattoo their hands. He said: As for these two, it is something you have innovated, and it did not exist in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so let the woman put it on at prayer. I said: Do you see every tattoo by which a woman increases beauty? He said: There is no good in it. I said: Her tattooing her lips then rubbing antimony into them? He said: There is no good in it."
"4949","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When a woman places hair on her head without joining? He said: Let her put it on when she stands for prayer, for it is an innovation."
"4950","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no harm in a woman placing hair on her head without joining. From Mamar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, he said: I saw Muawiya on the pulpit in Medina, and in his hand was a lock of hair, then he said something I do not remember now."
"4951","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he saw Muawiya preaching on the pulpit, and in his hand was a lock of hair. He said: I heard him say: Where are your scholars, O people of Medina? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid joining this, and say: The Children of Israel were only punished when they adopted it, the Children of Israel were only punished when their women adopted this."
"4952","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he heard Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan, with a lock of hair in his hand, say: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding the like of this, and saying: The Children of Israel were only punished when their women adopted this."
"4953","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rebuked a woman joining anything to her head."
"4954","Mamar informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from Fatima bint al-Mundhir, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, that a woman of the Ansar came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, we have married a young girl of ours, and she was ill so her hair fell out, shall we join hair for her? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has cursed the woman who joins hair and the one who has it joined."
"4955","From Mamar, from a man, from one who heard al-Hasan, that he disliked joining with wool."
"4956","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Ikrima, that he said: I was informed that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The women of the Children of Israel joined their hair, so Allah cursed them, and prevented them from entering Bayt al-Maqdis. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has cursed the woman who joins hair and the one who has it joined."
"4957","From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn Murra, from al-Harith al-Awar, from Ibn Masud, he said: The eater of usury, and its giver, and its scribe, and its witness when they know of it, and the tattooing woman and the one tattooed, and the withholder of charity, and the transgressor regarding it, and the drunkard, and the one who apostatizes as a Bedouin after his migration, are cursed upon the tongue of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of Resurrection."
"4958","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: Four are cursed: the tattooing woman, and the woman who files teeth, and the woman who plucks eyebrows, and the woman who joins hair."
"4959","From Mamar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about tattooing? He said: It is from the dress of the people of ignorance."
"4960","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, he said: Abdullah said: Allah has cursed the tattooing women, and the women who seek tattooing, and the women who pluck eyebrows, and the women who file teeth for beauty, those who change the creation of Allah. He said: That reached a woman of Banu Asad called Umm Yaqub, so she said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, it has reached me that you cursed such and such. He said: And why should I not curse one whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed, and who is in the Book of Allah? She said: I recite what is between the two covers and I do not find it. He said: If you were a reader you would have found it; have you not read: Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it, and whatever he forbids you, refrain? She said: Yes. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it. She said: I think your family does some of that. He said: Then go and look. He said: So she entered and did not see anything of her need. Then Abdullah said: If she were like that she would not have stayed with us. We said to Abu Bakr: What is the plucking woman? He said: The one who plucks her hair."
"4961","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from the wife of Ibn Abi al-Saqr, that she was with Aisha, and a woman asked her and said: O Mother of the Believers, there are hairs on my face, shall I pluck them to adorn myself thereby for my husband? Aisha said: Remove harm from yourself, and adorn yourself for your husband as you adorn yourself for a visit, and when he commands you, obey him, and when he swears upon you, fulfill it, and do not give permission in his house to one he dislikes."
"4962","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said: Do you see women who go out in the daytime, when they hear the call to prayer, is attending the prayer binding upon them? He said: If she likes she may come to it, and if she does not do so there is no sin. I said: His saying: O you who believe, when the call is made for prayer on Friday, is it not for women along with men? He said: No."
"4963","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: Is it binding upon women, when they hear the call to prayer, to respond as it is binding upon men? He said: No, by my life."
"4964","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not prevent the female servants of Allah from praying in the mosque. Then a son of Abdullah said: We do prevent them. He said: So he reviled him with a severe reviling, and said: We narrate to you from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and you say: We do prevent them."
"4965","From al-Thawri, from Layth and al-Amash, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give permission to women at night to go to the mosque. His son said: By Allah, we will not give them permission, lest they take that as deceit. He said: So he said to him: May Allah do to you, you hear me say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and you say: No? Layth said in his hadith: They should go out unattractive, wearing worn clothes, disheveled, without perfume."
"4966","Mamar informed us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muslim, the brother of al-Zuhri, informed me, from a freedwoman of Asma bint Abi Bakr, from Asma, she said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever among you believes in Allah and the Last Day, let her not raise her head until we raise our heads, lest they see the private parts of men due to the shortness of their waist-wrappers, and they at that time used to wear these striped cloaks."
"4967","From Mamar, from Aban, he said: A man asked Anas ibn Malik: Did women attend prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by Allah! Then why did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The best rows of women are the last row, and the worst rows of women are the first row, and the best rows of men are the first row, and the worst rows of men are the last row?"
"4968","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Atika bint Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, and she was under Umar ibn al-Khattab, used to attend prayer in the mosque, and Umar used to say to her: By Allah, you know I do not like this. She said: By Allah, I will not stop until you forbid me. He said: I will not forbid you. She said: Umar was stabbed on the day he was stabbed, and she was in the mosque."
"4969","Mamar informed us, from Ismail ibn Umayya, from Amra, from Aisha, she said: If the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had seen women today, he would have prevented them from going out."
"4970","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amra, from Aisha, she said: If the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had seen what women innovated after him, he would have prevented them from mosques, as the women of the Children of Israel were prevented. He said: I said: O so-and-so, were the women of the Children of Israel prevented? She said: Yes."
"4971","From Ibn Muammar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, she said: The women of the Children of Israel used to take legs of wood, showing themselves off to men in mosques, so Allah forbade mosques to them, and menstruation was imposed upon them."
"4972","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abu Mamar, from Ibn Masud, he said: Men and women among the Children of Israel used to pray together, and a woman who had a lover would wear two wooden molds to appear tall thereby to her lover, so menstruation was cast upon them. Ibn Masud used to say: Put them back where Allah put them back. We said to Abu Bakr: What are the two molds? He said: Two thin pieces of wood."
"4973","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, he said: The prayer of a woman in her house is better than her prayer in other than it. Then he said: When a woman goes out, Satan looks eagerly at her."
"4974","From al-Thawri, from his father, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, he said: A man came and said: It was said: The prayer of a woman in her house is better than her prayer in her courtyard. Abu Amr said to him: Why do you prolong? I heard the master of this house, meaning Ibn Masud, swear, emphasizing the oath: No place of prayer of a woman is better than her house, except in Hajj or Umra, except a woman who has despaired of husbands, for she is in her two sandals. It was said: What are her two sandals? Abu Bakr said: An old woman whose steps have become short."
"4975","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: He had three wives, none of them prayed in the mosque of the quarter."
"4976","From Mamar, from Ismail ibn Umayya, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the going out of women? He said: They go out unattractive."
"4977","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Anas, he said: They go out unattractive."
"4978","From Ibn Uyayna, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqama, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not prevent the female servants of Allah from the mosques of Allah, and let them not go out except unattractive."
"4979","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of your wives asks permission to go to the mosque, let him not prevent her. Ibn Uyayna said: And Abd al-Ghaffar narrated to us that he heard Abu Jafar with a report like that, from Ibn Umar, and Nafi, the freedman of Ibn Umar, said to him: That is only at night."
"4980","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi and Ata, they said: There is no harm in a man leading women in prayer."
"4981","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwa, that Umar ibn al-Khattab ordered Sulayman ibn Abi Hathma to lead the women at the back of the mosque in the month of Ramadan. Sufyan said: Our companions dislike that, and say: What if he loses his ablution, whom will he put forward? And they say: Voluntary prayer is easier."
"4982","From Muhammad ibn Umara, from Amr al-Thaqafi, from Arfaja, that Ali used to order the people to stand in prayer in the month of Ramadan, and appoint an imam for the men and an imam for the women. He said: So he ordered me and I led the women."
"4983","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Ayyub, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, he said: Mosques used to be built plain, and cities used to be elevated with high buildings."
"4984","From al-Thawri, from Abu Fazara, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, and he was the maternal cousin of Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have not been commanded to erect lofty mosques. He said: And Ibn Abbas said: By Allah, you will surely decorate them."
"4985","From al-Thawri, from Abu Fazara, from Muslim al-Batin, he said: Ali used to pass by a lofty mosque of Taym and say: This is the church of Taym."
"4986","From Ibn Saman, he said: Nafi informed me, from Ibn Umar, he said: The mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was built of bricks, its pillars were wood, and its roof was palm stalks. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away and Abu Bakr succeeded, and he did not alter it until he died. Then Umar succeeded and enlarged it, and made its pillars of wood as before, and roofed it with palm stalks. When Uthman came he enlarged it, built it with engraved stones, and roofed it with teak."
"4987","From Ibn Saman, he said: It reached me that it was revealed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to take a mosque as a thatch, like the thatch of Musa, rising a cubit into the sky."
"4988","From Ismail ibn Ayyash, from Husayn ibn Ubaydillah ibn Yasar, he said: One of our elders narrated to me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your mosques will be adorned as the Jews and Christians adorned their churches."
"4989","From Ismail ibn Ayyash, from Abu Uthman al-Qurashi, from Ali ibn Abi Talha, from Abu al-Darda, he said: When you decorate your copies of the Quran and adorn your mosques, destruction is upon you."
"4990","From Ismail, also, from al-Ala ibn al-Hajjaj, from Hawshab al-Tai, he said: No community did evil in its deeds except it adorned its mosques, and no community ever perished except through its scholars."
"4991","From al-Thawri, or other than him, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, that Ali said: When people decorate their mosques, their deeds become corrupt."
"4992","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, and others, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Khalid ibn Madan, that Ubayy ibn Kab and Abu al-Darda measured the mosque, then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the measure. He said: Rather a thatch like the thatch of Musa, reeds and wooden beams, for the matter is more urgent than that. Al-Thawri said: And it reached us that the thatch of Musa, when he stood, his head touched it."
"4993","From al-Thawri, from Qabus, from Abu Zabyan, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab entered the mosque and performed one raka, and it was said to him. He said: It is only voluntary, so whoever wishes adds and whoever wishes decreases; I disliked taking it as a road."
"4994","From al-Thawri, from Husayn, from Abd al-Ala, he said: I entered the mosque with Ibn Masud, and he bowed, and a man passed him while he was bowing and greeted him, and he said: Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth. When he finished, he said: It was said: Among the signs of the Hour are that a man greets a man only for acquaintance, and mosques are taken as roads, and women mount horses, and prices fall and never rise until the Day of Resurrection, and man and woman strip together."
"4995","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Sharik ibn Abi Namir, from Ata ibn Yasar, he said: Among the signs of the Hour are the raising of the voice of the wicked in mosques, and rain with no growth, and mosques taken as roads, and the appearance of children of adultery."
"4996","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, he said: I said to al-Hasan: Do you not dislike a man passing through the mosque and not praying in it? He said: Yes."
"4997","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, or other than him, that Ibn Masud said: Among the signs of the Hour are that a passerby passes a mosque and does not perform two rakas in it, and that a boy among boys sends an old man as a messenger between horizons, and that greeting be for acquaintance, and that shepherds of sheep, barefoot and naked, live in houses of mud brick."
"4998","Mamar informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Umara ibn Abd, he said: I heard Ali say: Yahya ibn Zakariyya was sent and ordered to tell his people five words, and to strike parables for them. They pleased him so he kept them to himself. It was said to Isa: Go to Yahya and order him to convey the words he was ordered; otherwise you convey them. When he came to him, he said: I shall convey them. He said to his people: The parable of associating partners with Allah is like a man who bought a slave with his wealth, treated him well and freed him, and said: Go and depart; then he earned favor and gave his favor and benefit to a man other than the one who freed him. That is the parable of associating partners with Allah. Prayer, its parable is like a man who came to an awesome ruler, not hoping he would allow him speech; then he came to him and he allowed him to say what he wished. That is the parable of one praying, when in prayer Allah gives him of his supplication what he loves. Zakat, its parable is like a man seized by the enemy, who said: Kill him, what are you waiting for? He said: What will you do by killing me? Rather set installments upon me, and I shall pay you the price of my neck. So they set installments upon him; whenever he paid an installment, part of his bondage was loosed until he was freed. Likewise charity expiates sins. The parable of fasting is like a man who witnessed battle and took up arms until he saw that nothing could reach him. That is the parable of fasting, a shield from the Fire. The Quran, its parable is like a people in a fortified fortress; the enemy comes to them only to find them wary. Likewise is the parable of the companion of the Quran against Satan."
"4999","Mamar said, and Yahya ibn Abi Kathir informed me something like this. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I command you with five: hearing, obedience, migration, unity, and jihad in the path of Allah. Whoever leaves unity by a handspan has cast Islam off his head until he returns. Whoever calls with a call of ignorance is among the throngs of Hell. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, even if he prays and fasts? He said: Yes, but call yourselves by the name of Allah by which He named you: Muslims, believers."
"5000","From Ibrahim ibn Umar ibn Kaysan, he said: Hafs ibn Maysara informed me, from a descendant of Hudhayfa, that he said: I was alone one day, wanting to strive in praising my Lord and supplicating, but I was tongue-tied. I heard a speaker say, though I saw no one: Say: O Allah, our Lord, to You is all praise, to You is all dominion, in Your hand is all good, to You returns all matter, open and secret. Worthy to be praised, You are over all things powerful. O Allah, forgive me all my past sins, protect me in the rest of my life, and grant me pure deeds with which You are pleased with me. He said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he said: That is an angel who taught you praise of your Lord and supplication."
"5001","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, he said: The drying of the earth is its purification. And may Allah bless Muhammad abundantly and grant him peace."
"5002","Abu Said Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Basri informed us, he said: Abu Yaqub Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Abbad al-Dabari narrated to us, he said: We read to Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: The people of Medina held Friday prayer before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived, and before Friday was revealed, and they are the ones who called it Friday. The Ansar said: The Jews have a day they gather in every seven days, and the Christians likewise. Come, let us make a day in which we gather and remember Allah, pray and thank Him, or as they said. They said: Saturday is for the Jews, and Sunday is for the Christians, so make it the day of Uruba, and they used to call Friday the day of Uruba. They gathered to Asad ibn Zurara, and he prayed with them that day and reminded them, and they called it Friday, until they gathered to him. Asad ibn Zurara slaughtered a sheep for them, and they ate lunch and dinner from one sheep, due to their fewness. Allah revealed concerning that after that: When the call is made for prayer on Friday, hasten to the remembrance of Allah."
"5003","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Who first held Friday prayer? He said: A man of Banu Abd al-Dar, they claim. I said: By the order of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Be quiet."
"5004","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Musab ibn Umayr ibn Hashim to the people of Medina to teach them the Quran. He asked permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to hold Friday prayer with them, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him permission, though he was not a ruler at the time, but he went to teach the people of Medina. Mamar said: Al-Zuhri used to say: Wherever there is a ruler, he admonishes his companions on Friday and prays two rakas with them."
"5005","From Said ibn al-Saib ibn Yasar, he said: Salih ibn Said al-Makki narrated to us that he was with Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz while he was in the desert at al-Suwayda, during his governorship over the Hijaz. He said: Friday came, and they prepared for him a seat of gravel. The caller to prayer called for prayer, and he went out to them and sat on that seat. Then they called another call, then he preached to them, then prayer was established and he prayed two rakas with them, reciting aloud in them. Then he said to them when he finished his prayer: The imam holds Friday prayer wherever he is."
"5006","From Mamar, from Ata al-Khurasani, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz came to Mecca for Hajj or Umra, and held Friday prayer with them while traveling."
"5007","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Maslama ibn Abd al-Malik wrote to him: I am in a village with much wealth, family and people; shall I hold Friday prayer with them though I am not a ruler? He wrote to him: Musab ibn Umayr asked permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to hold Friday prayer with the people of Medina, and he gave him permission, and they were few at the time. If you see fit to write to Hisham for him to give you permission, do so."
"5008","From al-Thawri, from his father, from Ibrahim, he said: Friday is attended from two farsakhs."
"5009","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: It reached me that the people of Dhu al-Hulayfa used to hold Friday prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Al-Zuhri said: That is six miles. Mamar said, and Qatada said: Two farsakhs."
"5010","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, and from Qatada, from al-Hasan, they said: Friday is binding upon one whom the night shelters returning to his family."
"5011","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I asked Ata: From where is Friday attended? He said: Ten miles to a barid."
"5012","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me that people used to come down for prayer on Friday from four or six miles."
"5013","Dawud ibn Qays informed us, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb was asked, while I listened: From where is Friday attended? He said: From the reach of the voice."
"5014","From a man of Aslam, from Uthman ibn Muhammad, that he sent to Ibn al-Musayyab asking him: Upon whom is Friday binding? He said: Upon one who hears the call."
"5015","From Hisham ibn Urwa, from Aisha bint Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, she said: My father would be six or eight miles from Medina; he would sometimes attend Friday in Medina, and sometimes not attend it."
"5016","Mamar informed us, from Thabit al-Bunani, he said: Anas would be on his land, with three miles between him and Basra, and he would attend Friday in Basra."
"5017","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, that Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As would be at al-Waht and not attend Friday with the people at Taif, though between him and Taif were four or three miles."
"5018","From a man of Aslam, from Abu al-Zinad, from Abu Maymuna al-Asadi, he said: Abu Hurayra would be five miles from Medina, and he would hold Friday prayer and come down."
"5019","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed me that Muawiya used to call people to attend Friday on the pulpit at Damascus, saying: Attend Friday, O people of such-and-such, O people of such-and-such, until he called the people of Matrin and the people of Fain, then eight and twenty miles from Damascus, saying: Attend, O people of Faiz."
"5020","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: Abda ibn Abi Lubaba informed me that Muadh ibn Jabal used to stand on his pulpit and say: O people of Qurada, and O people of Damira, two villages of Damascus, one at four farsakhs and the other at five: Friday is binding upon you, and there is no Friday except with us."
"5021","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: It reached us that men of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who had witnessed Badr lost their sight in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and after him, yet they did not leave attending Friday. We do not think one who finds a way to it should leave Friday."
"5022","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: O you who believe, when the call is made for prayer on Friday, are women not included with men? He said: No. And we asked Abd al-Razzaq: From where is it recommended that Friday be attended? He said: From the village of al-Rahba to Sana, and the like of this; what is farther than that, if they wish they attend, and if they wish they do not attend."
"5023","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from a man of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I know only that he raised the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Whoever hears the call three Fridays then does not attend is written among the hypocrites."
"5024","From Ibrahim ibn Abi Yazid, that he heard Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Jafar say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is it for one of you to take an estate of a lifetime at a mile or two from Medina, or three, then Friday comes and he does not attend it, then Friday comes and he does not attend it, so Allah seals his heart. From Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, each of them, from a man, from Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Jafar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5025","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Mina, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, while on the steps of the pulpit: People will surely stop neglecting Friday, or Allah will surely seal their hearts and they will surely be written among the heedless. Mamar said: Sometimes he said: Al-Hakam ibn Mina, from Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas, or one of them."
"5026","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, he said: Awf al-Abdi informed us that he heard Said ibn Abi al-Hasan say: I heard Ibn Abbas say: Whoever leaves Friday four consecutive Fridays without excuse has cast Islam behind his back."
"5027","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have intended to order a man to pray with the people, then go and burn down the houses of people who do not attend Friday. From Mamar, he said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me, mentioning from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except he said: I order my young men to gather bundles of firewood."
"5028","From Mamar, from Qatada, he said: He once stayed back from Friday, and it was said to him. He said: This mud and mire prevented me. Mamar said: Qatada used to say: Meeting people returning from Hajj, having missed it, is dearer to me than meeting them returning from Friday."
"5029","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from al-Hakam, he said: Whoever leaves Friday a single day has no expiation short of the Day of Resurrection."
"5030","From Mamar, from a man of the people of Medina, that Abu Hurayra said: Drinking a cup of wine, or he said: an ounce, is dearer to me than deliberately leaving Friday."
"5031","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: No Friday, and no tashriq, except in a congregational city. From al-Thawri, he said: Jabir informed us, from Sad ibn Ubayda, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Ali, the like of that, adding: And no seclusion except in a congregational mosque."
"5032","From al-Thawri, from Zubayd, from Sad ibn Ubayda, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Ali, he said: No Friday, and no tashriq, except in a congregational city. And he used to count the cities: Basra, Kufa, Medina, Bahrayn, Egypt, Syria, Jazira, and sometimes he said: Yemen and Yamama. From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: Wasit is a city."
"5033","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is a congregational town? He said: One with a community, a ruler, retribution, and houses joined together, not scattered, some holding to others, like Jeddah. He said: And retribution, he said: Jeddah is congregational, and Taif. He said: When you are in a congregational town, and the call is made for prayer on Friday, it is binding upon you to attend it, whether you hear the call or not."
"5034","From Mamar, from Said ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jahshi, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, that he ordered the people of Quba, and the people of Dhu al-Hulayfa, and the people of small villages around him: Do not hold Friday prayer, and attend Friday in Medina."
"5035","He said: Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to the people of the waters between Mecca and Medina to hold Friday prayer. Ata said at that: It has reached us that there is no Friday except in a congregational city."
"5036","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held Friday prayer with his companions on a journey, and preached to them leaning on a bow."
"5037","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: We heard that there is no Friday except in a congregational town."
"5038","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, he said: I heard Amr ibn Dinar say: When the mosque is one in which prayer is held in congregation, Friday shall be prayed in it."
"5039","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar would see the people of the waters between Mecca and Medina holding Friday prayer, and would not blame them."
"5040","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Arafa, al-Zahran, Sarif, and the people of this valley of ours are not congregational."
"5041","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When you are in a non-congregational town, and its people hold Friday prayer, if you wish join them, and if you wish do not, unless you hear the call. If you join them, when the imam gives salutation in two rakas, add two rakas, and do not shorten with them."
"5042","He said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: I asked him about a non-congregational town whose people hold Friday prayer and shorten prayer. He said: I said: Shall I join them and shorten? He said: Yes."
"5043","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa said: No Friday, no Adha, and no Fitr, except with one who attends the imam."
"5044","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you see the people of Basra, whom the great mosque does not hold, what shall they do? He said: Each people has a mosque in which they hold Friday prayer, then that suffices them. Ibn Jurayj said: People disapproved of that, that they hold Friday prayer except in the great mosque."
"5045","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I prayed Friday with a man who did not preach, and prayed four, and I deemed him mistaken. When I asked about that, he turned out to be right."
"5046","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, he said: He prayed with an imam who did not preach on Friday, and the imam prayed two rakas. He said: Al-Dahhak stood and prayed two rakas after finishing the prayer, making them four. Sufyan said, and others said: He begins the prayer as four, and does not count what he prayed with the imam."
"5047","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that he disliked for the imam of a non-congregational town to preach then pray four. He said: Ata, when the imam did not preach on Friday, would pray four."
"5048","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Said, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim: When the imam does not preach on Friday, he prays four."
"5049","Said said, and Qatada informed us, from al-Hasan, that he said: He prays two rakas in every case."
"5050","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Friday is not binding upon women and slaves."
"5051","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Friday is not binding upon the traveler."
"5052","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: He would not bathe on Friday while traveling. And he used to say: The traveler has no Friday."
"5053","From al-Thawri, from al-Mujalid ibn Said, from al-Shabi, he said: Friday is not binding upon the woman, nor the slave, nor the traveler, nor the boy."
"5054","From Mamar, from Layth, from Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever was upon unlawful wealth, and Allah turned away from him and moved him from it to other than it, that Allah forgive him. Whoever does good to a doer of good, believer or disbeliever, his reward falls upon Allah, in the present of his world or the future of his Hereafter. Whoever prays a prayer, ten are prayed upon him. Whoever calls a blessing upon me, his sins are removed from him. Friday is binding upon every Muslim, or he said: Whoever believes in Allah, Friday is binding upon him, except a slave, a woman, a boy, or a sick person. Whoever is sufficed by diversion or trade, Allah is sufficed of him, and Allah is Rich, Praiseworthy."
"5055","Mamar informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, that he saw Ibn Masud expelling women from the mosque, saying: Go out to your houses, it is better for you."
"5056","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: They would not hold Friday prayer on a journey, and would pray only two rakas."
"5057","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The traveler has no Friday."
"5058","Mamar informed us, from Qatada, he said: Whatever slave pays land tax must attend Friday; if he has no tax upon him, or his master's work occupies him, there is no Friday upon him."
"5059","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I asked him about a traveler passing a town, alighting in it on Friday? He said: When he hears the call, let him attend Friday."
"5060","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: The traveler has no Friday."
"5061","Mamar informed us, and al-Thawri, from Layth, from Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Women and slaves have no Friday."
"5062","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Every festival is when the forenoon grows intense: Friday, Adha, and Fitr. Thus it reached us."
"5063","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydillah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I came early on Friday; when the sun declined Umar came out, ascended the pulpit, and the caller to prayer began his call."
"5064","From Mamar, from Jafar ibn Burqan, from Thabit Abu al-Hajjaj, from Abdullah ibn Saydan, he said: I attended Friday with Abu Bakr, and he finished his prayer and sermon before midday. Then I attended Friday with Umar, and he finished his prayer and sermon at the declining of the sun."
"5065","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Yazid ibn Hurmuz, he said: Aban ibn Uthman informed me, he said: We used to pray Friday with Uthman, then return and take a midday rest."
"5066","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It reached me that Uthman used to hold Friday prayer, then people would rest after prayer."
"5067","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musab ibn Shayba ibn Jubayr informed me that he heard Umar ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Rabia informing al-Walid ibn Abd al-Malik, he said: We used to hold Friday prayer with Nafi ibn Abd al-Malik in the Hijr. Ata said: That has reached us."
"5068","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, he said: Muadh ibn Jabal came from Syria and found the people of Mecca praying Friday in the Hijr, and he scolded them not to pray it until the Kaba casts shade from its face, and that is the declining of the sun."
"5069","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Said ibn Jafar informed me that his father informed him that he found Utba ibn Abi Sufyan holding Friday prayer with the people in the Hijr in intense daylight, standing on the ground with nothing beneath him."
"5070","From Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Ismail ibn Sumay al-Hanafi, from Abu Razin, he said: We used to hold Friday prayer with Ali ibn Abi Talib, then leave, and there would sometimes be shade and sometimes no shade that we see. He says: We see it sometimes, and sometimes we do not see it."
"5071","From Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Ismail ibn Sumay, from a man he named, he said: We used to hold Friday prayer with Ammar ibn Yasir, and I do not know whether the sun had declined or not."
"5072","From al-Thawri, from Jabir al-Jufi, from Mujahid, he said: When prayer is intended, he said: The resolve is at the reminder, meaning: when he preaches."
"5073","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from a man, from Masruq, he said: When the call is made for prayer, he said: That is the time."
"5074","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: We used to hold Friday prayer with Ibn Masud, then return and take a midday rest."
"5075","A man informed us, from al-Harith, from Fudayl, from Muhammad ibn Kab, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Friday with us when the least shade fell."
"5076","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: When the first imam calls the adhan, all crafts become forbidden; they are in the same position as buying and selling."
"5077","From al-Thawri, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, he said: When the call is made for prayer on Friday, when the sun has passed its zenith, buying and selling becomes forbidden."
"5078","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, he said: The adhan in which buying and selling becomes forbidden is the adhan at the coming out of the imam. Al-Zuhri said: And I hold that trade should now be left at the first adhan."
"5079","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: When the call is made for prayer on Friday, buying and selling become forbidden."
"5080","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayya, he said: If a man buys after the sun has passed its zenith, the sale is void. And he used to say: Every worker with his hand, when the sun has passed its zenith, should not work."
"5081","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Ikrima ibn Khalid, he said: I came out of the mosque and Muslim ibn Nawfal met me on Friday and said: Have you prayed? I said: No. He said: I prayed it with Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he placed the pulpit in the Hijr."
"5082","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If there is a village that is not a congregational town, they should not pray Friday until the sun has declined and risen high at the time of Zuhr then."
"5083","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you know anything that becomes forbidden when the first adhan is called besides trade? He said: Yes, crafts. I said: A book, if a man wants to write it then? He said: Nothing at all. I said: Goods he wants to prepare? He said: No. I said: If a man wants to take a midday rest then? He said: Neither sleep, nor approaching his wife then when the first adhan is called. I said: When the first adhan is called, does going early become obligatory at that moment? He said: Yes. I said: Because of His saying, When the call is made for prayer on Friday? He said: Yes, so let him leave everything then and go early."
"5084","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is raising the voice in recitation on Friday established practice? He said: Yes."
"5085","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated from his father, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi, he said: Abu Hurayra used to lead us in Friday prayer, reciting in the first rak'a Surah al-Jumu'a and in the second When the hypocrites come to you. Ubayd Allah said: I met Abu Hurayra when he left and said: O Abu Hurayra, I heard you recite two surahs that Ali ibn Abi Talib used to recite in Kufa. Abu Hurayra said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite them, and Abu Bakr follows that."
"5086","From al-Thawri, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Abu Rafi: That Ali used to recite in Friday prayer Surah al-Jumu'a and When the hypocrites come to you. He said: I mentioned that to Abu Hurayra, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do that."
"5087","From Ma'mar, from Jabir ibn Yazid al-Ju'fi, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, from Abu Hurayra, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repeating these two surahs in Friday prayer: Surah al-Jumu'a and When the hypocrites come to you."
"5088","From al-Thawri, from Mikhwal, from Muslim al-Batin, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite on Friday at Fajr Alif Lam Mim, the Revelation of the Prostration, and Has there come upon man, and he used to recite in Friday prayer Surah al-Jumu'a and When the hypocrites come to you."
"5089","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, from Habib ibn Salim, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the two Eids and on Friday: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and Has there reached you the story of the Overwhelming."
"5090","From Ibn Uyayna, from Damra ibn Sa'id, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba, he said: al-Dahhak ibn Qays wrote to al-Nu'man ibn Bashir asking him with what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite on Friday along with Surah al-Jumu'a? He said: Has there reached you the story of the Overwhelming."
"5091","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited in Friday prayer Surah al-Jumu'a and O Prophet, when you divorce women."
"5092","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in Friday prayer Surah al-Jumu'a and Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and in Fajr prayer on Friday Alif Lam Mim, the Revelation, and Blessed is He in Whose Hand is dominion."
"5093","From al-Thawri, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayra: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite on Friday at Fajr Alif Lam Mim, the Revelation of the Prostration, and Has there come upon man. Abd al-Razzaq said: And we follow this."
"5094","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in Fajr prayer Alif Lam Mim, the Revelation, and a surah from the Mufassal, and sometimes he said: Has there come upon man."
"5095","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Umar ibn Ata ibn Abi al-Khuwar say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My pulpit is upon a meadow from the meadows of Paradise; whoever swears beside it over a green tooth-stick falsely, let him take his seat in the Fire. Let your present convey to your absent."
"5096","Al-Thawri reported from Ammar al-Duhni, from Abu Salama, from Umm Salama: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The legs of my pulpit are fixed in Paradise."
"5097","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Hafs ibn Asim, from Abu Hurayra: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is between my house and my pulpit is a meadow from the meadows of Paradise, and my pulpit is upon my Pond."
"5098","From a man of Aslam, from Salih, freedman of al-Taw'ama: That Baqul, freedman of al-As ibn Umayya, made for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his pulpit of tamarisk wood with three steps. When Mu'awiya came to Medina he added to it, and the sun eclipsed at that time."
"5099","From Ibn Jurayj, from Zayd ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Abbad, from Abd Allah ibn Zayd: That he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: What is between my pulpit and my house is a meadow from the meadows of Paradise."
"5100","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stand when he preached upon a staff? He said: Yes, he used to lean upon it firmly."
"5101","Ibn Jurayj said: Umar ibn Ata narrated to me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had taken a palm stalk from date-palm fronds with which he silenced the people and gestured; then Allah revealed to him: O Muhammad, why do you break the horns of your subjects? So he cast it away. Jibril and Mika'il came to him, and Mika'il said: Your Lord gives you the choice to be a king-prophet or a servant-prophet. He looked at Jibril, who gestured with his hand: Be humble. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather a servant-prophet. Jibril said: You are the chief of the children of Adam, and you are the first for whom the earth will split, and the first to intercede."
"5102","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: An angel came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Your Lord gives you the choice between being a servant-prophet or a king-prophet. He looked at Jibril as if consulting him, and he gestured to him: Be humble. He said: Rather a servant-prophet. After that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was never seen eating reclining. Al-Zuhri said: The angel had not come to him before that nor after."
"5103","Jafar ibn Muhammad told me, from his father: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to carry a straight palm-stalk from the daughters of Tab; Sufyan said: It is a straight stalk, and if there is crookedness in it, it is straightened. He said: With that stalk he struck Sawada ibn Ghaziyya al-Ansari, who said: O Messenger of Allah, retaliation. He said: Yes. That distressed the people; they said: O Messenger of Allah, he is needy, he only wants you to give him something. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) let him take retaliation, and he kissed between his eyes; then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him something after that. As for Ma'mar, he reported from a man, from al-Hasan, that he said: Sawada ibn Amr."
"5104","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: Jibril, blessings of Allah upon them both, or an angel, came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a stick. He said: Do not break the horns of your community."
"5105","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a palm stalk of date-palm with which he silenced the people; then Allah revealed to him: O Muhammad, do not break the horns of your community. The stalk was never seen with him after."
"5106","From a man of Aslam, from Abu Jabir al-Bayadi, from Ibn al-Musayyib: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to lean on a staff while preaching on Friday when he preached leaning to the trunk; when the pulpit was made he stood upon it and also leaned on the staff."
"5107","From al-Aslami, from Abu Jabir, from Ibn al-Musayyib: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Abd Allah ibn Unays al-Sulami a staff and said: Take this and carry it at your side, and know that those who carry at the side on the Day of Resurrection are few. He said: When Abd Allah ibn Unays died, that staff was buried with him."
"5108","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from a man he named, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to stand on Friday beside a date-palm trunk set up in the mosque and preach, until it occurred to him to take a pulpit; he consulted men of judgment among the Muslims, and they held he should take it. So he took a pulpit. When Friday came, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walked until he sat on the pulpit. When the trunk missed him it moaned with a moan that startled the people. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose from his seat until he came to it and stood by it and wiped it, and it calmed; no moaning was heard from it after that. Ma'mar said: And I heard one saying: Had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not done to it what he did, it would have moaned until the Day of Resurrection."
"5109","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr told me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached, he leaned against a trunk from the pillars of the mosque. When his pulpit was made and he settled upon it, that pillar shook like the moaning of a she-camel, until the people of the mosque heard it, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came down to it and embraced it, and it fell silent."
"5110","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked a man of the people of Medina: What happened to the trunk to which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to stand when he preached? He said: It was buried in the mosque."
"5111","From al-Thawri, from Simak ibn Harb, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Samura say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to sit between the two sermons on Friday and preach; his prayer was moderate, his sermon was moderate, and he recited verses of the Quran upon the pulpit."
"5112","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Samura say: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preach on Friday standing, then sit without speaking, then stand and preach another sermon. Whoever tells you that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached sitting has lied."
"5113","From Ma'mar, from Qatada: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman used to preach on Friday standing. Then Uthman did that until standing became hard for him, so he would preach standing, then sit, then stand again and preach. When Mu'awiya came, he preached the first sitting, then would stand and preach the last standing."
"5114","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman used to preach on Friday standing, not sitting except for the interval between the two sermons; the first who sat was Mu'awiya. When Abd al-Malik came, he preached standing and struck his foot on the pulpit and said: This is the practice. When the matter grew long upon him, he sat afterwards."
"5115","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The sermon of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Friday was standing, twice with a sitting between them. I said: Has that reached you from a trustworthy one? He said: Yes, as you wish."
"5116","Ma'mar reported from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to preach on Friday twice with a sitting between them."
"5117","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Qaza'a told me, he said: Trembling seized Uthman, so when he stood on the pulpit he would rest a while, then stand and preach."
"5118","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali told me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Friday, when he settled on the pulpit, would sit; when he sat, the muezzins would call the adhan; when they fell silent he would stand and preach; when he finished the first sermon he would sit, then stand and preach the last sermon."
"5119","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad told me, from his father, he said: When Mu'awiya came, he sought the people's permission to sit in one of the two sermons, saying: I have grown old and wish to sit in one of the two sermons; so he sat in the first sermon."
"5120","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never sat on a pulpit until he died; he never preached except standing. How you people wish to make them feel: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, one of them would ascend the pulpit and stand, not sitting on the pulpit until he ascended it, nor sitting on it afterwards until he descended; his whole sermon was standing. They would testify only once, the first. There was no pulpit except the pulpit of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until Mu'awiya came when he performed Hajj with his pulpit and left it; they continued to preach on pulpits."
"5121","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Who first put sitting in the sermon? He said: Uthman, at the end of his time when he grew old and trembling seized him; he would sit a little while then stand. I said: Did he preach while sitting? He said: I do not know."
"5122","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, he said: Abu Ishaq told me, he said: I went out with my father to Friday while I was a boy. When Ali came out and ascended the pulpit, my father said: O Amr, so I stood, and he was standing on the pulpit, white of head and beard, wearing a waist-wrap and cloak with no shirt on him. He said: I never saw him sit on the pulpit until he came down from it. I said to Abu Ishaq: Did he recite Qunut? He said: No."
"5123","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: How did Ibn al-Zubayr preach? He said: He would sit and preach sitting, then stand and preach also; his sitting was most of that."
"5124","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: I saw Abu Mahdhura, when Khalid ibn Sa'id emerged from the gate of Banu Makhzum on Friday, call the adhan the moment he emerged; Khalid would not reach his place where he preached before Abu Mahdhura had finished. He said: And so did those who passed before do."
"5125","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata told me, he said: I saw Khalid ibn al-As preach standing on the ground, leaning against the House, with no sitting in between, neither before nor after, a single sermon, until Khalid fell ill and would sit on a ladder. He said: And so they used to preach standing on the ground, except the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon his pulpit."
"5126","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, he said: Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan preached nearly a year standing, then it was said to him: You seek the blood of Uthman yet oppose him; so he preached standing and sitting."
"5127","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I see that when the imams come down from the pulpit they touch the Corner before coming to the standing place; has anything reached you about it? He said: No. I said: Do you approve it? He said: No, except that touching the Corner, the more you do it, the better it is."
"5128","From al-Thawri, from Harun ibn Antara, from a man of Banu Fazara, from a woman of them, she said: Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud came to us on Friday and said: When you women pray with the imam on Friday, pray two rak'as; and when you pray in your houses, pray four. Sufyan said: And the slave is in the same position. From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from Shu'ba, from Amr ibn Murra, from Humayd al-Fazari, from a woman of them, the like of it, with an addition; he said: No year comes upon you but it is worse than the one before it; the death of my household one by one is easier for me than the death of their number of dung-beetles; you are only afflicted through your rulers; and what a bad servant of Allah I am if I lie."
"5129","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: When women attend Friday, they pray two rak'as."
"5130","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Women make up Friday prayer even if they are in the courtyard adjoining the mosque."
"5131","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not raise his hands in supplication."
"5132","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I asked him about raising the hands on Friday? He said: It is an innovation; the first who introduced it was Abd al-Malik."
"5133","From al-Thawri, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umara ibn Ruwayba al-Thaqafi, he said: He saw Bishr ibn Marwan raising his hands on Friday and reviled him, saying: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day saying only like this, and he pointed with his forefinger."
"5134","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abd Allah ibn Murra, from Masruq, he said: He saw them raising their hands on Friday while the imam preached, and said: O Allah, cut off their hands."
"5135","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he ascended the pulpit, turned his face to the people and said: Peace be upon you."
"5136","From Abu Usama, that he heard Mujalid narrating, from al-Sha'bi, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended the pulpit, he turned to the people with his face and said: Peace be upon you. He said: Abu Bakr and Umar used to do that after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5137","From Ma'mar, from Harun ibn Antara, from his father, from Ali: That he used to recite on Friday upon the pulpit: Say, O disbelievers, and Say, He is Allah, One."
"5138","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh: That Ammar ibn Yasir recited on the pulpit on Friday: When the sky splits asunder, then came down and prostrated."
"5139","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: I heard Hudhayfa on Friday, while upon the pulpit, recite: The Hour has drawn near and the moon has split; then he said: The Hour has drawn near and the moon has split; today is training, tomorrow is the race."
"5140","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: When the sky splits asunder, while upon the pulpit; when he reached the prostration in it, he came down and prostrated, and the people prostrated with him."
"5141","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, they said: There is no Qunut in Friday prayer. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan told me he says the like of that."
"5142","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: Qunut in the two rak'as of Friday? He said: I have not heard of Qunut in a prescribed prayer except in Fajr; and he denied that there is Qunut in Friday."
"5143","From al-Thawri, from a man he named, from Ibrahim, he said: Raising the hands and Qunut in Friday prayer is an innovation."
"5144","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while upon the pulpit, say: Whoever of you comes to Friday, let him bathe. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab told me, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5145","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father: That Umar ibn al-Khattab, while standing preaching on Friday, a man of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered; Umar called to him: What hour is this? He said: I was busy today and did not return to my family until I heard the call; I did no more than perform ablution. Umar said: And ablution also, though you know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command bathing. Ma'mar said: The man was Uthman ibn Affan."
"5146","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Sirin, he said: While Umar preached, a man entered; Umar said: What detained you? He said: O Commander of the Faithful, I did no more when I heard the call than perform ablution, then came. When the prayer was finished, Ibn Abbas said to him: Did you not hear what the Commander of the Faithful said? He said: Yet he knew that we were commanded to bathe. He said: I said: The Emigrants particularly or people generally? He said: I do not know."
"5147","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me that Ikrima, freedman of Ibn Abbas, told him that Uthman came while Umar preached on Friday; Umar turned aside to where the man sits until he finished the remembrance; Uthman said: O Commander of the Faithful, it was only that I heard the first call, performed ablution, and came out. Umar said: You knew it is not mere ablution, mere ablution."
"5148","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim told me, from Tawus, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is a right upon every Muslim who has reached puberty to purify himself on one day in every seven days for Allah; even if he is not junub, let him wash his head and skin on Friday. Al-Thawri said to a man: Trim your nails. The man said: Tomorrow is Friday, I will trim them. Al-Thawri said: Trim them now; the practice is not to be put off."
"5149","From al-Thawri, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from a man of the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is a right upon every Muslim to bathe one day in every seven days, on Friday, to clean his teeth, and to apply of his family's perfume. This is the more beloved of the two sayings to Sufyan; he says: It is obligatory."
"5150","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, and sometimes he said: From Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, he said: It is binding upon every adult to bathe one day in every seven days, washing his head and the rest of his body."
"5151","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me that he heard Tawus say: Abu Hurayra said: It is upon every Muslim for Allah to bathe one day in every seven days, washing everything of himself, and touching perfume if his family has it."
"5152","From Ma'mar, from Abu Layla, or Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, he said: I met the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), those of them who witnessed Badr or pledged under the tree: when Friday came and one of them wished to go early, he would bathe as he bathes from major impurity, wear his good clothes, and touch perfume if he had it."
"5153","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Ibn Ajlan, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, he said: Whoever trims his nails on Friday, clips his mustache, and cleans his teeth has completed Friday."
"5154","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: One whom I do not suspect told me, from the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that they heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on one of the Fridays, while upon the pulpit, say: O company of Muslims, this is a day Allah has made a festival for the Muslims; so bathe in it with water, and whoever has perfume, it harms him not to touch of it; and upon you is this tooth-stick."
"5155","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata told me that he heard Ibn Abbas asked about bathing on Friday? He said: Bathe; and if your family has perfume, it harms you not to apply of it. Ata said: Without one who leaves it being sinful. He said: I said to Ata: Do you dislike leaving it that day when you find it? He said: Yes."
"5156","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysara told me, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, that he mentioned the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about bathing on Friday. He said: I said to Ibn Abbas: And touching perfume or oil if his family has it? He said: I do not know it."
"5157","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata, saying to him: Is bathing on Friday obligatory? He said: Yes."
"5158","From Malik ibn Anas, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayra, he said: Bathing on Friday is obligatory like the bath from major impurity. A man said to him: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: No, and he grew angry."
"5159","From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: He would not go early to Friday without oiling himself and perfuming, unless he was in pilgrim sanctity."
"5160","From Ibn Uyayna, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made bathing on Friday obligatory upon every adult."
"5161","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la, from Suwayd ibn Ghafala, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, for something he said: I would then be more helpless than one who does not bathe on Friday."
"5162","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Mujahid, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, he said: I love to bathe from five: from the bathhouse, major impurity, cupping, shaving the pubes, and Friday. He said: I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: They did not hold any bath obligatory except the bath from major impurity, and they approved the bath of Friday."
"5163","From a man of the people of Basra, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah told him, from Humayd al-Himyari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever trims his nails on Friday, Allah removes ailment from him and brings cure to him."
"5164","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs ablution on Friday, with it and blessing; and whoever bathes, that is better."
"5165","From al-Thawri, from Ikrima ibn Ammar, from Yazid ibn Aban al-Raqashi, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs ablution on Friday, with it and blessing; and whoever bathes, that is better. From al-Thawri, from a man, from Abu Nadra, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5166","From Ma'mar, from one who heard Ikrima say: Whoever does not bathe on Friday, let him wash thoroughly, meaning washing his joints."
"5167","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amra, from A'isha, she said: People were workers of themselves; so it was said: If only you would bathe."
"5168","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mis'ar, from Wabara, from Hammam ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: Bathing on Friday is a practice."
"5169","From Fudayl ibn Iyad, from Layth, from Nafi: That Ibn Umar used to bathe for major impurity and Friday with a single bath."
"5170","From Umar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama, he said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: Three are upon every Muslim on Friday: bathing, the tooth-stick, and touching perfume if he finds it."
"5171","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from al-Zuhri, from Qatada and Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: They used to approve for a man, when he bathed at the start of the day on Friday then nullified, to make another bath. Al-Zuhri said: Because of the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever of you comes to Friday, let him bathe."
"5172","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, he said: It was approved to make a fresh bath with which he prays Friday."
"5173","Hisham said, and al-Hasan said: When one bathes on Friday after dawn has risen, that suffices him for Friday; if he nullifies, let him perform ablution."
"5174","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When one bathes at the start of the day on Friday before going early, then nullifies, ablution suffices him."
"5175","From Fudayl, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: When a man bathes on Friday after dawn has risen, that suffices him; if he nullifies, he performs ablution."
"5176","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abda ibn Abi Lubaba, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father: That he would nullify his ablution on Friday after bathing, then perform ablution and not repeat the bath."
"5177","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: That he would not bathe while traveling on Friday."
"5178","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqama: That he would not bathe on Friday while traveling, nor pray Duha while traveling."
"5179","From al-Thawri, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: I never saw him bathing at all while traveling on Friday."
"5180","From Ibn Mubarak, from Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talha, from al-Musayyib ibn Rafi, from Ziyad ibn Hudayr, he said: I was with Talha ibn Abd Allah on a journey; when Friday came he ordered me, so I screened him and he bathed, saying: Screen me from the direction of the qibla. He said: Then he screened me and I bathed."
"5181","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Tawus, Ata, and Mujahid: They used to bathe while traveling on Friday. Layth said: And a man told me that Sa'id ibn Jubayr used to bathe while traveling where he was brought as a captive."
"5182","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayya, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hibban: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why does not one of you take two garments for his Friday besides his two work garments? He said: And they used to wear striped cloaks. Abd Allah ibn Salam said: So I sold a striped cloak of mine and bought a figured one, meaning garments of Bahrayn."
"5183","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hayyan, he said: People used to come to the congregation with one striped cloak or two upon one of them; he would tie them upon himself. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is amiss with one of you, or what is amiss with you, when he finds means, to take two garments for his Friday besides his two work garments?"
"5184","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad told me, from his father: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He used to wear on every festival day a Yemeni cloak of hibara of his."
"5185","From a man, from Salih, from Muhammad ibn Za'ida, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The practice of Friday is bathing, the tooth-stick, perfume, and wearing your cleanest clothes."
"5186","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: Take your adornment at every mosque, he said: It is clothes. He said: And Tawus said: It is the wrap, part of adornment."
"5187","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If I go early in the morning on Friday, do I leave the midday rest? He said: If it is winter, no; if summer, yes, until the shadows turn."
"5188","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, from Tawus, he said: Friday is all prayer. He says: One prays at midday for Allah. Ma'mar said: I kept hearing that from others saying: Prayer until Asr."
"5189","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, from Tawus, he said: Friday is all prayer."
"5190","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ubayd ibn Abi Bakra, he said: It was said: When a man enters the mosque on Friday, let him say: O Allah, make me the best of those who turn to You, the nearest of those who draw near to You, the most successful of those who ask You and seek of You. He said: And it was said: The best of people on Friday are those who most send blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5191","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, he said: It reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Send many blessings upon me on Friday."
"5192","From Ibn Jurayj, Ibn al-A'rabi doubted, he said: Ata told us, he said: The adhan on Friday in the past was only one, then the iqama; that adhan was called when the imam emerged, so the imam would not stand upright where he preaches until the muezzin finished, or about that; that is when trade becomes forbidden, that is when the first adhan is called. As for the adhan now called before the imam comes out and sits on the pulpit, it is void. The first who introduced it was al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf."
"5193","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa said: The first who added the adhan in Medina was Uthman. Ata said: No, he only called people with a call, and called no more than one adhan."
"5194","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me that Uthman was the first who added the first adhan on Friday; when people grew many, he added it, and it was called upon al-Zawra. He said: As for the first who added it in our land, it was al-Hajjaj."
"5195","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The adhan on Friday in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, and Umar was a single adhan until the imam came out; when Uthman came and people grew many, he added the first adhan, intending people to prepare for Friday."
"5196","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, he said: The adhan in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Friday was a single adhan, when the imam came out, then the prayer was established after the sermon."
"5197","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I saw Ibn al-Zubayr: no adhan was called for him until he sat on the pulpit, and no more than one adhan was called for him on Friday."
"5198","From al-Thawri about a man who came after the imam had prayed on Friday, he said: He establishes the prayer, for he prays other than the imam's prayer."
"5199","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: In the reading of Ibn Mas'ud: Proceed to the remembrance of Allah, and it is like His saying: Your striving is diverse. Ma'mar said: And I heard another say: When you are engaged in it, you are engaged in it. He says: When you are engaged in it preparing for it, you are hastening to it."
"5200","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: His saying: Hasten to the remembrance of Allah. He said: Going is walking."
"5201","From Ma'mar and others, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar died reciting this verse in Surah al-Jumu'a only as: Proceed to the remembrance of Allah."
"5202","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud said: When the call is made for prayer on Friday, hasten to the remembrance of Allah. Abd Allah said: Had I recited it as hasten, I would hasten until my cloak fell; and he used to recite it: Proceed."
"5203","From al-Thawri, from Hanzala, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab used to recite it: Proceed to the remembrance of Allah."
"5204","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The coming out of the imam cuts off prayer; his speaking cuts off speech."
"5205","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, he said: Tha'laba ibn Abi Malik al-Qurazi told me, he said: Umar would come and sit on the pulpit while the muezzin called the adhan, and we would talk; when the muezzin finished his adhan, our talk stopped."
"5206","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about people's speech when the imam comes down and before prayer, and he said: There is no harm in that. A man with him disapproved of that. Al-Zuhri said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be spoken to when he came down from the sermon."
"5207","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I saw Ata speak when the imam comes down and before prayer."
"5208","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: I asked Alqama: When is speech disliked on Friday? He said: When the imam preaches, or he said: When the imam comes out, he doubted. I said: What do you hold about a man reciting to himself? He said: Perhaps that does not harm him."
"5209","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: That he used to forbid speech after the imam's coming down from the pulpit and before prayer. And he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysara told me that he spoke to Tawus after the imam's coming down and before prayer, and he spoke to him."
"5210","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara: That Tawus spoke to them after Sulayman ibn Abd al-Malik came down on Friday."
"5211","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Uqba: That Ibn Abbas and Sa'id ibn Zayd spoke on Friday after the imam had come out and before he preached, while beside the pulpit, with Umar upon the pulpit."
"5212","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: There is no harm in speech on Friday while the imam is on the pulpit and the muezzin calls the adhan. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan told me, he says: Silence is approved."
"5213","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When the imam comes out on Friday and you are praying, do not sit until the imam sits. He said: I said: The imam came out and I am praying standing; does it harm me not to sit while he walks if he does not sit and I am standing? He said: No."
"5214","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: There is no harm in speech while the imam sits on the pulpit and the muezzins call the adhan; listening is not obligatory until the imam speaks."
"5215","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri and Qatada about two men entering the mosque while the imam preaches on Friday, he said: They may speak in the mosque so long as they have not sat. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, the like of it."
"5216","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from Abu Umayya al-Thaqafi, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar used to pray on Friday; when he sensed the imam's coming out, he would sit before he came out."
"5217","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I heard him narrate from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: People at Friday are three: a man who attends it with stillness, dignity, and listening; that is he for whom what is between the two Fridays is forgiven. He said: I reckoned, he said: And an increase of three days. He said: And a witness who attends it with idle talk; that is his share of it. And a man who prays after the imam has come out; it is not the practice; if He wills He gives him, and if He wills He withholds from him."
"5218","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Rabi'a ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman told me that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended the pulpit on Friday, he said: Sit. An Ansari man heard the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while on the road, not yet entered the mosque, and sat among Banu Ghanm. He said: When the prayer was established, the man entered; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Did you not go early? He told him the story; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Good. They claimed that man was Abd Allah ibn Rawaha."
"5219","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, he said: It reached me that Ibn Rawaha heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while on the road, say: Sit. So he sat on the road; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him and said to him: What is the matter with you? He said: I heard you say: Sit, so I sat. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: May Allah increase you in obedience."
"5220","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: While the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached, he said: Sit. Ibn Mas'ud heard him and sat at the door of the mosque, inside the mosque; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Come, O Abd Allah."
"5221","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What made silence obligatory on Friday? He said: His saying: And when the Quran is recited, listen to it and be silent. He said: So they held this regarding prayer and regarding Friday. He said: I said: Is silence for one listening to the sermon like silence for one listening to the Quran? He said: Yes."
"5222","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I glorify Allah and declare His oneness on Friday while I comprehend the sermon? He said: No, except a small amount, and keep it between you and yourself. It was said to him: May a person remember Allah while the imam is preaching on the day of Arafah, or on the day of Fitr, while he comprehends the imam's speech? He said: No, all of that is Eid, so do not speak except if the imam turns to something other than the remembrance of Allah."
"5223","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When the imam prays for rain, supplicate; he is commanding you at that time."
"5224","Mamar reported from Qatadah, that Uthman said: The reward of the silent one who does not hear the sermon is like the reward of the silent one who hears the sermon."
"5225","From Malik ibn Anas, from Abu al-Nadr, from Malik ibn Abi Amir, from Uthman ibn Affan, he said: He used to say in his sermon, seldom omitting that the imam addresses when he stands: Listen and be silent, for the silent one who does not hear any of the sermon has the same as the listening silent one. Then when the prayer is established, straighten the rows and align the shoulders, for straightening the row is part of completing the prayer. Then he would not say takbir until men came to him, whom he had entrusted with straightening the rows, and they informed him that they were straight, then he would say takbir."
"5226","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim: I recite my portion when I do not listen to the sermon on Friday."
"5227","From Mamar, he said: al-Zuhri was asked about glorification and takbir while the imam is preaching. He said: Silence was commanded. He said: I said: The imam has turned to something other than the remembrance of Allah on Friday? He said: Speak if you wish. Mamar said: And Qatadah said: If they introduce new matters, do not introduce them."
"5228","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If I do not hear the imam, may I declare oneness, say takbir, glorify, supplicate Allah, and supplicate for my family naming them, and name my debtor? He said: Yes. He said: I said: Even if the imam has not supplicated? He said: Yes."
"5229","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Speech is forbidden so long as the imam is on the pulpit, even if he turns to other than that; Allah is remembered."
"5230","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, he said: I heard Tawus say: When it is Friday and the imam is preaching on the pulpit, no one should supplicate for anything, nor remember Allah, except if the imam remembers."
"5231","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that he disliked on Friday, while the imam is on the pulpit, fidgeting, moving, and yawning. He said: And people can hardly avoid that on Friday, due to the length of the sermon."
"5232","Mamar informed me, he said: One who heard Ikrimah narrated to me, that he forbade turning over pebbles and cracking fingers on Friday while the imam is preaching."
"5233","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Hilal ibn Qays, from Zayd ibn Sawhan, he said: When you come to Friday, be silent and do not fiddle with pebbles; if a man near you is speaking, nudge him, and if he is far, gesture to him."
"5234","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, he said: When Abdullah ibn Atik al-Ansari and his companions killed Sallam ibn Abi al-Huqayq al-Awar of the Jews, they entered the mosque while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was preaching on Friday. When he saw them he said: Successful are the faces."
"5235","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that Umar said, while on the pulpit: Knead the dough well, for it is the better of the two grindings; or he said: the better of the two flours. Hisham said: He saw it as a duty upon him to command them with what he commanded his family."
"5236","From Hushaym ibn Bashir, he said: Muhammad ibn Qays informed me, that he heard Musa ibn Talhah say: I saw Uthman sitting on the pulpit on Friday, while the muezzins were calling the adhan, and he was asking the people about their prices and their news."
"5237","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Did they avoid mixing the sermon with anything except the remembrance of Allah Almighty? He said: Yes. I said: Did they pronounce the tashahhud in the sermons on Friday? He said: Yes. I said: Then why were we called those who praise? He said: We say: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds. I said: And praying for rain or praying for healing? He said: No harm."
"5238","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: After everything the imam says on the pulpit, if he commands good or forbids evil, sale or purchase, measure or weight, it is remembrance."
"5239","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to supplicate on the pulpit on Friday and the people would say amen. He said: And Ata said: It is a recent matter, and it is good."
"5240","Ibn Uyaynah reported from Misar, from Imran ibn Musa, from Abu al-Sabah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said to a man while on the pulpit on Friday: Did you buy for us? Did you bring us this? And he pointed with a fingertip of his fingers, meaning grain."
"5241","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The people speaking to the ruler while he preaches, singling him out with talk or asking him about some matter of remembrance? He said: I dislike that. He said: I said: Then the people speaking to the imam while he is on the pulpit, praising him? He said: And I dislike it; Friday is only remembrance."
"5242","From Mamar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about the people facing the imam on Friday, and he said: So they used to do."
"5243","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar used to face the imam on Friday."
"5244","From al-Thawri, from Tawbah, from al-Shabi, that Shurayh used to face the imam on Friday."
"5245","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The people facing the imam on Friday, and the storyteller in Makkah and elsewhere, they leave the House? He said: Yes. Then he informed me at that time from one who informed him, from Yala ibn Umayyah, that Ubayd ibn Umayr came to narrate here, and he pointed toward the side of Banu Makhzum, while Sinan ibn Yala or Said ibn Yala was facing the House, so Yala called him and said: What carried you to do what you did? Face the remembrance. Then Abbad ibn Abi Abbad said at that time: It is Sinan ibn Yala."
"5246","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Then one who is level with the pulpit, does he face the imam and leave the House? He said: Yes, he faces the House."
"5247","From Mamar, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, he said: I was sitting with Ibn Umar, and the people were asking him while Ubayd ibn Umayr was narrating, so Ibn Umar said: Leave us and our reminder alone."
"5248","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah informed me, he said: I entered the mosque and prayed Asr with Ibn Umar, then he sat and his companions gathered around him, and he turned his back toward the storyteller. He said: Then he spoke at length. He said: Then the storyteller raised his hand supplicating, but Ibn Umar did not raise his hand."
"5249","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Then the stories of this storyteller, other than the imam's sermon on Friday, may I remember Allah while I hear him and comprehend him? He said: Yes, and sit with him as long as you wish, and stand when you wish, and raise your voice with some remembrance. I said: Then if a person sneezes and praises Allah, do I respond with mercy? He said: Yes, by my life. I said: May I converse, I and another person, while we hear him? He said: Yes, and that you glorify and remember is more beloved to me."
"5250","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Has it reached you that silence is not obligatory at the battle line? He said: Yes, by my life, indeed it is obligatory. Then he recited: When you meet those who disbelieve advancing, do not turn them your backs, and remember. He said: So remembrance became obligatory at that time. He said: And there is no talk at that time except remembrance. I said: Do you raise your voices with remembrance? He said: Yes."
"5251","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When the imam comes out on Friday, separate with speech before he preaches. I said: The imam greeted, and I returned it; is that a separation? He said: I like that you add also, besides the speech of greeting, speech about the Quran."
"5252","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The first who narrated stories was Tamim al-Dari in the time of Umar; he sought his permission for one standing every Friday and he permitted him, so he would stand. He said: Then he asked him for another standing and he granted it. Then in Uthman's time he asked him for another standing, so he would narrate on Friday three times."
"5253","Mamar said: And I heard other than al-Zuhri say: When Umar passed by him while he was narrating, he passed the sword over his throat."
"5254","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, that Ibn Umar used to leave the mosque, and a man would meet him and say: What is the matter with you, O Abu Abd al-Rahman? And he would say: The storyteller drove me out."
"5255","Mamar said: al-Zuhri said: And Ibn al-Musayyib would hear them reciting the prostration verse, but would not prostrate, and would say: I did not sit with them."
"5256","From Mamar, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Abdullah ibn Iyad, he said: Ubayd ibn Umayr entered upon Aishah, and she asked: Who is this? He said: I am Ubayd ibn Umayr. She said: Umayr ibn Qatadah? He said: Yes, O my mother. She said: Has it not reached me that you sit and people sit with you? He said: Yes, O Mother of the Believers. She said: Then beware of making people despair and destroying them."
"5257","From Mamar, he said: One who heard al-Hasan narrating informed me, he says in his stories: True is the one who says: He who died and rested is not dead; the dead is only the dead of the living. Mamar said: And I saw Ata al-Khurasani narrating by the Sunan."
"5258","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar would not sit with the storytellers, except the storyteller of the congregation."
"5259","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid and others, he said: I saw Ibn Umar raise his hands with the storyteller. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I saw him, meaning Mamar, do it."
"5260","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Aishah sent to Marwan complaining of al-Saib, and he was a storyteller, and she said: By Allah, I cannot speak to my servant. So Marwan forbade him, then he returned, so she complained of him again, so Marwan met him again and struck him, or he said: slapped him."
"5261","From Mamar, he said: It reached me that Ali passed by a storyteller and said: Do you know the abrogating from the abrogated? He said: No. He said: You have perished and caused perdition. He said: And he passed by another and said: What is your kunyah? He said: Abu Yahya. He said: Rather you are Abu Irafuni."
"5262","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Bayan, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: A storyteller who sits at night and says to the people: Say such, say such, was mentioned to Ibn Masud, and he said: When you see him, inform me. So they informed him. He said: Then Abdullah came masked and said: Whoever knows me has known me, and whoever does not know me, I am Abdullah ibn Masud. Do you know that you are better guided than Muhammad and his companions, or that you are clinging to the tail of misguidance."
"5263","From Jafar, he said: Ata ibn al-Saib informed us, he said: I know it only from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: It reached Abdullah ibn Masud that people sit from Maghrib to Isha glorifying, saying: Say such, say such. Abdullah said: If they sit, inform me of them. When they sat they informed him, so he went when they informed him, entered and sat with them, wearing a hooded cloak. They began their glorification, so Abdullah uncovered the hood from his head and said: I am Abdullah ibn Masud. The people fell silent, and he said: You have come with a dark innovation, or you have surpassed the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in knowledge. He said: A man of Banu Tamim said: We have not come with a dark innovation, nor surpassed the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in knowledge. Amr ibn Utbah ibn Farqad said: I seek Allah's forgiveness, O Ibn Masud, and repent to Him. He said: Then he ordered them to disperse. And Ibn Masud saw two circles in the mosque of Kufah and said: Which of you two was before the other? One of them said: We. He said to the other: Move to them, and made them one."
"5264","From Mamar, from Ata ibn al-Saib, he said: Ibn Masud heard of people going out to the desert with a storyteller saying: Glorify. Then he said: I am Abdullah ibn Masud, and you have surpassed the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in knowledge, or you have come with a dark innovation. If you have taken their path, they have preceded far ahead; and if you have opposed them, you have gone far astray. Upon what do you enumerate the command of Allah?"
"5265","From a man of the family of Hazm, he said: Abu Bakr looked at a storyteller who had prolonged, and said: If it were said to this one: Stand and pray two rakahs, recite in them such and such, he would find that tiresome."
"5266","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: The sermon on Friday is given, whether short or long."
"5267","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The sermon on Friday is before the prayer."
"5268","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: If you say to your companion: Be silent, while the imam is preaching, you have engaged in idle talk. Ibn Shihab said: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to me, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Qariz, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5269","From Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: If you say to your companion: Be silent, while the imam is preaching on Friday, you have engaged in idle talk. From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Utbah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of the first hadith of Ibn Jurayj."
"5270","From Mamar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you say to the people: Be silent, on Friday while they are speaking and the imam is preaching, you have engaged in idle talk against yourself."
"5271","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata al-Khurasani, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If he says: Hush, he has engaged in idle talk, and if he engages in idle talk he has cut off his Friday."
"5272","From Umar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever catches the sermon has caught Friday, and whoever does not catch the sermon has caught the prayer. Whoever draws near the imam, listens and is silent, will have two shares of reward; and whoever does not listen nor is silent will have two shares of sin. And whoever says: Hush, while the imam is preaching, has engaged in idle talk; and whoever engages in idle talk, there is no Friday for him; or he said: there is nothing for him."
"5273","From Mamar, he said: Amr and others informed me, from al-Hasan, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited a verse on Friday, and Ibn Masud said: O Ubayy ibn Kab, do you recite it like this? Ubayy kept silent from him, and they were at Friday. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, Ubayy said to Ibn Masud: You did not attend Friday today. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ubayy spoke the truth."
"5274","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you know anything that breaks the Friday of Islam, so that he must pray four from speech, or stepping over people's necks, or other than that? He said: No."
"5275","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It is said: Whoever speaks, his speech is his share of Friday, meaning: of the reward of Friday; as for completing it as four, no."
"5276","From Ibn Shurayh, from a man, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: While the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on the pulpit on Friday, when he recited a verse, Abu Dharr heard it and Abu Dharr said to Ubayy ibn Kab: When was this verse revealed? Ubayy kept silent from him three times, each time keeping silent from him, until when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended, Ubayy said to Abu Dharr: There is nothing of your Friday for you except what had passed of it. Abu Dharr asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he said: Ubayy spoke the truth."
"5277","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me that he heard Tawus say: He considers it idle that a man gesture to a man with his hand to be silent when he speaks."
"5278","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar pelted two men who were speaking while the imam was preaching on Friday. From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the like of the hadith of Ibn Umar about the two men speaking."
"5279","From Mamar, from Ayyub, that Ibn Umar saw a beggar begging while the imam was preaching on Friday, and he pelted him."
"5280","Mamar reported from Ayyub, from Nafi, that he saw Ibn Umar gesture to a man on Friday while the imam was preaching."
"5281","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hasan, that he saw a beggar while the imam was preaching, and gestured with his hand to be silent."
"5282","From al-Thawri, from Muslim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: I saw him gesture to Muhammad ibn Sad while al-Hajjaj was preaching, and he was speaking, so he gestured to him to be silent."
"5283","From al-Thawri, from Mujalid ibn Said, he said: I saw Amir al-Shabi and Abu Burdah speaking while al-Hajjaj was preaching, when he said: May Allah curse the liars, and may Allah curse. I said: Do you two speak while the imam is preaching? They said: We were not commanded to be silent for this one."
"5284","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, he said: I saw Ibrahim al-Nakhai speak to a man while the imam was preaching on Friday in the time of al-Hajjaj."
"5285","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, he said: A man may drink water when thirsty while the imam is preaching on Friday."
"5286","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: If a person sneezes on Friday and praises Allah, and you hear him and hear the sermon, do not respond with mercy; and even if you do not hear the sermon, do not respond."
"5287","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If a person sneezes on Friday while the imam is preaching and praises Allah, and you hear him and hear the sermon, respond to him within yourself; if you do not hear the sermon, respond and let him hear."
"5288","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, about a man who sneezes on Friday, he said: Respond to him with mercy."
"5289","From Israil ibn Yunus, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, he said: I witnessed Amir al-Shabi responding to the sneezer while the imam was preaching on Friday."
"5290","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah ibn Said ibn Abi Hind, he said: My father sent me to Ibn al-Musayyib to ask him about a man who sneezes on Friday while the imam preaches Friday, shall I respond? He said: No."
"5291","From Mamar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, about a man greeting a man while he is in the sermon, they said: He returns it to him and lets him hear."
"5292","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Israil, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, from al-Shabi, he said: A man does not return greeting while the imam is preaching on Friday."
"5293","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi and Salim ibn Abdullah, they said: He returns the greeting while the imam is preaching. Jabir said: And al-Qasim ibn Muhammad said: You return the greeting within yourself. And Abd al-Razzaq holds by this."
"5294","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If a man greets on Friday while the imam is preaching, if you hear the sermon, return it within yourself; and if you do not hear the sermon, return it and let him hear."
"5295","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: If the pages are read on Friday, do not speak to anyone; if they innovate, do not innovate."
"5296","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he disliked the reading of the pages on Friday. He said: If they are read, do not speak. He said: And the reading of the pages on Friday is a new matter they introduced."
"5297","From Ibn Jurayj, from one who narrated to him, that Said ibn Jubayr used to speak when the pages were read on Friday."
"5298","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said: If the pages are read and I am at the pulpit, hearing its recitation, may I glorify, declare oneness, remember Allah within myself, supplicate for my family naming them by their names, and say: O Allah, extract for me from my debtor, naming him by his name? He said: Yes."
"5299","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he disliked that a man lean on Friday while the imam is preaching, except due to ailment, old age, or sickness."
"5300","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: When the imam prolonged the sermon, Ibn Umar would lean on me."
"5301","From a man of Aslam, from Salih, freedman of al-Tawamah, that Abu Hurayrah used to lean on him on Friday while the imam was preaching."
"5302","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: For one who did not attend the sermon, but heard it as Friday, then sat in the shade and withdrew from the reminder? He said: Glory be to Allah, yes; why would he have no Friday? He went out to Allah seeking only Allah. Ata said: And if he draws near him, it is more beloved to me; if he endures the sun, it is better for him."
"5303","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The muezzins sit in the minaret over the mosque and do not sit with the people; do they fall short? He said: Yes. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I asked Mamar about it, and he said: They fall short."
"5304","Mamar reported from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Abu Qatadah, he said: Whoever does not pray Friday in the mosque, there is no Friday for him. Mamar said: If compelled, al-Hasan saw no harm in praying it in the road or in the courtyard of the mosque; wherever compelled by constriction or crowding, let him pray two rakahs. He said: So we say to al-Hasan: It is the dung of beasts. And he says: He prays."
"5305","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: I and my father came once and found the mosque full on Friday, so we prayed with the prayer of the people in a house by the mosque, with a road between them. He said: I thought he said: In the house of Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman."
"5306","From a man, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Suhayl, from Salih ibn Ibrahim, that he saw Anas ibn Malik pray Friday in the house of Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman with the prayer of the imam, with a road between them."
"5307","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah, he said: I and Zirr prayed, and he led me, and Friday had passed me, so I asked Ibrahim? And he said: Abdullah did that with Alqamah and al-Aswad. Sufyan said: And perhaps I and al-Amash did it."
"5308","From al-Thawri, from a man, from al-Hasan, that he disliked, when people did not catch Friday, that they pray in congregation. And the saying of Sufyan is more beloved to me. Abd al-Razzaq said: And by it we take."
"5309","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, that he disliked that they pray Friday in congregation. And Abd al-Razzaq also holds by this."
"5310","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, that Zayd ibn Thabit came to the mosque on Friday and met the people departing, so he entered a house and prayed in it. It was said to him: Why did you not come to the mosque? He said: One who is not ashamed before people is not ashamed before Allah."
"5311","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: He used to command one who missed Friday to go to the mosque and pray in it."
"5312","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Shubrumah, that a man met the people on Friday having departed, and Hudhayfah said to him: Avoid the paths of the people, for there is no good in one who has no modesty in him."
"5313","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Whoever cannot pray on Friday due to crowding, he prays four rakahs when they crowd and he cannot bow nor prostrate. Mamar said: And one who heard al-Hasan informed me, he says: He bows two rakahs, for he entered with them in their prayer."
"5314","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, he said: If you wish, prostrate on a man's back; and if you wish, when the imam stands, prostrate. And Abd al-Razzaq holds by this."
"5315","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: A man prostrates on a man's back when he finds no place to prostrate upon."
"5316","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Shabi, that Umar said: When crowding is severe on Friday, let one of you prostrate on his brother's back."
"5317","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Fudayl, from Ibrahim, he said: Umar said: When heat harms one of you on Friday, let him prostrate on his garment."
"5318","From al-Thawri, from al-Ala, from Mujahid, he said: When crowding is severe, prostrate on a man's leg. Sufyan said: If you cannot prostrate on a man's leg, stand until the people stand, then you prostrated."
"5319","From al-Thawri, he said: When people crowd on Friday, and a man is crowded so he neither bowed nor prostrated, while standing, then when he finds room, he need only bow and prostrate; he is in the position of the sleeper, and the imam's recitation suffices him."
"5320","From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Musayyib ibn Rafi, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever is oppressed by heat on Friday in the mosque, let him pray on his garment; and whoever is crowded by people, let him prostrate on his brother's back."
"5321","From Mamar, from Khasif al-Jazari, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar, he said: When a man catches one rakah on Friday, he prays another rakah added to it."
"5322","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar said: When a man catches one rakah on Friday, he prays another rakah added to it; if he finds them sitting, he prays four. And Abd al-Razzaq holds by this. From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the like of it, and by it we also take. From al-Thawri, from al-Ashath, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the like of it. From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, the like of the hadith of Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar. From Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from Alqamah and al-Aswad, the like of it also. From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, the like of it. He said: And one who heard al-Hasan informed me, he says the like of that."
"5323","From Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, he said: Whoever catches the rakah has caught Friday, and whoever does not catch the rakah, let him pray four."
"5324","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever catches a rakah of the prayer has caught the prayer. al-Zuhri said: So Friday is part of prayer."
"5325","Mamar reported from Abu Ishaq, from Hubayrah ibn Yarim, from Ibn Masud, he said: Whoever misses the last rakah, let him pray four."
"5326","From Mamar, from Hammad, he said: When he catches them sitting at the end of the prayer on Friday, he prays two rakahs. Mamar said: Qatadah said: He prays four. It was said to Qatadah: Ibn Masud came to them sitting at the end of the prayer, and said to his companions: Sit; you have caught, if Allah wills. Qatadah said: He only means: You have caught the reward."
"5327","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: When a man catches the imam on Friday while he is sitting, not having saluted, let him pray with his prayer two rakahs; he is in the position of the traveler. al-Thawri said: And four is more pleasing to us, because Friday has passed him."
"5328","Mamar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Nadrah, he said: A man came to Imran ibn al-Husayn and said: A man has missed Friday; how much does he pray? Imran said: Why does Friday pass him? When the man turned away, Imran said: As for me, if Friday passed me, I would pray four."
"5329","From Jafar, he said: I heard Abu Ghalib say: I heard Abu Umamah, companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), say: When it is Friday, the angels stand at the doors of the mosque and write the people according to their stations, the first. If a man of them delays from his station, the angels supplicate for him, saying: O Allah, if he is sick, heal him; O Allah, if he has a need, fulfill his need for him. They remain so until when the imam comes out, the pages are folded then sealed. Whoever comes after the descent of the imam has caught the prayer but not caught Friday."
"5330","From Umar ibn Rashid and others, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever catches the sermon has caught the prayer."
"5331","From al-Awzai, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: I heard him say: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The sermon is in place of the two rakahs; whoever misses the sermon prays four."
"5332","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is it that if a person catches it on Friday he shortens, and otherwise he completes the prayer? He said: The sermon. He said: I said: Then I did not sit until the imam descended? He said: He did not catch the imam. He said: I said: Then I sat before he descended? He said: It suffices you; you have caught."
"5333","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A person said to Ata: I did not catch the sermon, except while he was on measure and balance? He said: Allah commanded that, so that is part of remembrance, so shorten."
"5334","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata and Mujahid, they said: Whoever does not catch the sermon prays four."
"5335","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, about a man who bled from the nose while the imam was preaching, so he stood, performed ablution, and did not return until the imam prayed and finished, he said: He prays two rakahs; he attended the sermon."
"5336","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: He prays four rakahs. And al-Thawri said: He prays four. And Abd al-Razzaq holds by this."
"5337","From al-Thawri, about a man who did not witness the sermon, and came when the imam stood in prayer, then the imam had a ritual impurity and wanted to put him forward, he said: Do not put forward except one who witnessed the sermon. If he had prayed with the imam part of his prayer, there is no harm in putting him forward; let him pray the completion of two rakahs. And the imam who had the impurity, then returned: if he had spoken, he prays four; and if he had not spoken, he prays two. If the imam puts forward a man who witnessed nothing with the imam of his sermon nor his prayer, he prays four."
"5338","From al-Thawri, about a man who prayed with the imam one rakah on Friday, then had a ritual impurity and left, and did not speak? He said: Yes, he performs ablution and completes what remains; if he spoke, he prays four."
"5339","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: You were at the pulpit while the imam was preaching, and you were called for help over a child; would you stand to him and leave Friday? He said: Yes. I said: Then a child, a brother, and a cousin? He said: I would not stand except for good or kinship, and worldly life did not distract me from Friday."
"5340","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Umar: He was called for help over Said ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl on Friday after the day had risen high, so he went out to him and did not attend Friday that day."
"5341","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ismail ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Dhuayb al-Asadi, that Ibn Umar was called to Said ibn Zayd while he was dying, and Ibn Umar was perfuming himself standing for Friday, so he went to him and left Friday. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ismail ibn Abd al-Rahman, the like of it."
"5342","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar was called for help over Said ibn Zayd on Friday after forenoon had risen high, so Ibn Umar came to him at al-Aqiq."
"5343","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, that a man came stepping over people's necks while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was preaching. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his sermon and his prayer, he said: O so-and-so, did you attend Friday today? He said: Did you not see me, O Messenger of Allah? He said: I saw you, and you harmed and delayed. From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from al-Walid ibn Abdullah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5344","From Mamar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, they said: If you see a gap before you, before the imam comes out, there is no harm in coming to it without harming anyone."
"5345","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If I see a gap before me with people short of it, shall I step over them to it? He said: No. I said: What if I slip through them slipping? He said: And how? I said: As if two men are not touching. He said: Yes, if you do not step over anyone. A person said to him: Then there were two men touching at their knees; shall I step over their knees? He said: No."
"5346","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Shall I step over people's necks sitting while the imam has not come out? He said: No. I said: They were standing praying, and the imam had not come out; shall I slip through the people? He said: If you do not lift anyone, nor harm him, and do not precede anyone, then yes; if there is any of that, do not harm anyone."
"5347","From Abu Said, that he heard Makhul say: The first row on Friday, and the foremost row in the path of Allah, are like for like; whoever removes a man from his place will have his reward."
"5348","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, that he heard Ibn al-Musayyib say: That I attend Friday at al-Rawha is more beloved to me than that I step over people's necks on Friday."
"5349","From a man, from Salih, freedman of al-Tawamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I would not like red camels for myself with my leaving Friday; and that I fill it at the back of the lava field is more beloved to me than that I step over people's necks when they have taken their seats. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Ajlan, from Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, the like of it."
"5350","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A person asked Makhul, while I was listening and he was sitting with Ata, about Allah's saying: The believers are only those who believe in Allah, until His saying: And when they are with him upon a collective matter, this verse. Makhul said: It is acted upon now; one should not go on Friday, nor at the battle line, until he seeks the imam's permission. He said: And likewise in a collective matter. Do you not see Him say: And when they are with him upon a collective matter. Ata said at that: I have indeed met people in the past, by my life, seeking the imam's permission when they stood while he was preaching. I said: How did you see them seek permission? He said: A man gestures with his hand. Ata gestured to me with his right hand. I said: He gestures and does not speak? He said: Yes. I said: The imam, when he permits? He said: He gestures and does not speak. I said: And a person does not place his hand on his nose, nor on his garment? He said: No."
"5351","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, about His saying: And when they are with him upon a collective matter, he said: About Friday. Mamar said: And I heard Qatadah say: About Friday, and about expedition also."
"5352","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Ibn Sirin, he said: People used to seek permission on Friday, saying: Like this, gesturing with three fingers. When Ziyad came, they became many upon him and he grieved, so he said: Whoever holds his nose, that is his permission."
"5353","From Mamar, from al-Kalbi, he said: When a man had a need on Friday while the imam was preaching, and wanted to leave, and something hastened him, he would place his hand on his nose, then leave."
"5354","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, about His saying: And when they are with him upon a collective matter, he said: About expedition, and about Friday. And the imam's permission on Friday is that he gesture with his hand."
"5355","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Sad al-Ama informed us, that a man of the Ansar came on Friday while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was preaching, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you bow? He said: No. He said: Then bow two rakahs."
"5356","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: A man came while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Friday was on the pulpit preaching, and he said to him: Did you bow two rakahs? He said: No. He said: Then bow. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: Those two rakahs are for no one, except for a man for whom the imam cut his sermon and commanded him with that."
"5357","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: A man called Sulayk of Ghatafan came while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was preaching standing, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Sulayk, stand and bow two light rakahs."
"5358","From al-Thawri, from Rabi, from al-Hasan, he said: I saw him pray two rakahs while the imam was preaching on Friday. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Ajlan, from Iyad ibn Abdullah ibn Sad ibn Abi Sarh, from Abu Said al-Khudri, the like of the hadith of Ibn Jurayj, from Abu Sad al-Ama."
"5359","From al-Thawri, from Abu Nuhayk, from Simak al-Hanafi, from Ibn Abbas, he said: They asked him about a man who prays while the imam is preaching? He said: What if all of them did that, would it be good?"
"5360","From al-Thawri, from Tawbah, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, he said: When it is Friday he comes to the mosque; if the imam has not come out, he prays two rakahs; and if he has come out, he does not pray, and sits on his haunches, faces the imam, and does not turn right nor left."
"5361","From Mamar, he said: I asked Qatadah about a man who comes while the imam is preaching on Friday, and he had not prayed, shall he pray? He said: As for me, I would sit."
"5362","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: You came while the imam is preaching on Friday, do you bow? He said: As for while the imam is preaching, I would not bow."
"5363","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: It reached me that you bow twelve rakahs before Friday; what reached you about that? He said: Umm Habibah daughter of Abu Sufyan informed Anbasah ibn Abi Sufyan, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever bows twelve rakahs, a house is built for him in Paradise."
"5364","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that he saw Ibn Umar pray after Friday. He said: He moves a little from his prayer place, bows two rakahs, then walks a bit further than that, then bows four rakahs."
"5365","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq and al-Zubayr, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: I saw Ibn Umar, when he finished the Friday prayer, advance a little from his prayer place and bow two rakahs, then advance also and bow four."
"5366","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Ibn Mas'ud used to pray four rak'ahs before al-Jumu'ah and four rak'ahs after it."
"5367","Abu Ishaq said: And Ali used to pray six rak'ahs after al-Jumu'ah, and Abd al-Razzaq adopts this view."
"5368","From al-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: Abdullah used to command us to pray four before al-Jumu'ah and four after it, until Ali came to us and commanded us to pray two rak'ahs after it, then four."
"5369","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray two rak'ahs after al-Jumu'ah in his house. Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likewise."
"5370","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that Amr ibn Shu'ayb prayed al-Jumu'ah then prayed two rak'ahs right after it in the mosque, so Ibn Umar forbade him that and said: As for the imam, then no; when you have prayed, then turn away and pray in your house whatever you wish, except that you circumambulate, and as for the people, they pray in the mosque."
"5371","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever among you prays after al-Jumu'ah, let him pray four."
"5372","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin or another, that Imran ibn Husayn prayed al-Jumu'ah with Ziyad, then stood and prayed four after it, so the people said: He did not count Ziyad's prayer as valid. That reached Imran, so he said: For daggers to pierce my belly is dearer to me than to do that. Then when the next Jumu'ah came, he prayed al-Jumu'ah with him, then sat and did not pray anything until he prayed al-Asr."
"5373","From al-Thawri, from Nusayr ibn Dhu'luq, from Muslim ibn Iyad, he said: I said to al-Hasan ibn Ali: Are the two rak'ahs of al-Jumu'ah made up like others? He said: Yes."
"5374","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: ... the imam ... the mosque, should he pray in it by night or by day? He said: Yes, good."
"5375","From Ma'mar, from one who heard Ikrimah saying: When you have prayed al-Jumu'ah, do not join it with two light rak'ahs until you separate between them by moving or speaking."
"5376","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Umar ibn Ata ibn Abi al-Khuwar informed me that Nafi' ibn Jubayr sent him to al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid asking him about something Mu'awiyah saw him do in the prayer. He said: I prayed al-Jumu'ah with him in the maqsurah, then when he gave the salam I stood in my place and prayed, then when he entered he sent for me and said: Do not repeat what you did; when you have prayed al-Jumu'ah, do not join it to a prayer until you speak or go out, for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that, and we adopt it."
"5377","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Ibn Umar saw a man praying in the spot in which he had prayed al-Jumu'ah, so he forbade him and said: Why do I see you praying in your spot. Ma'mar said: Qatadah said: I mentioned that to Ibn al-Musayyib, and he said: That is only disliked for the imam who leads."
"5378","From Ma'mar, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Ibn Sirin or another, that Umar ibn al-Khattab saw a man wearing journey clothes after al-Jumu'ah was over and said: What is your matter? He said: I intended a journey, but I disliked going out until I pray, so Umar said to him: Al-Jumu'ah does not prevent you from traveling, so long as its time has not come."
"5379","From al-Thawri, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from his father, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab saw a man with travel gear, and the man said: Today is Friday, and were it not for that I would have gone out, so Umar said: Al-Jumu'ah does not detain a traveler, so go out so long as the time of going has not come."
"5380","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Muhammad ibn Umar, from Salih ibn Kaysan, he said: Abu Ubaydah went out on one of his journeys in the early morning on Friday and did not wait for the prayer."
"5381","From Ma'mar, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, that Salim ibn Abdullah went out from Makkah on Friday."
"5382","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Salih ibn Kathir, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out traveling on Friday in the forenoon before the prayer."
"5383","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: May a man go out on Friday? He disliked it, so I began narrating to him the concession about it, and he said to me: Seldom does a man go out on Friday except that he sees what he dislikes, and if you looked into that you would find it so."
"5384","Ibn al-Mubarak reported from al-Awza'i, from Hassan ibn Atiyyah, he said: When a man travels on Friday, the day supplicates against him that he not be helped in his need nor accompanied in his journey. Al-Awza'i said: And a man informed me from Ibn al-Musayyib that he said: Travel on Friday is after the prayer."
"5385","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said: Has it reached you that it used to be said: When one spends the night in a congregational town on the eve of Friday, he should not leave until he observes Jumu'ah? He said: That is disliked. I said: What about Thursday? He said: No, that day, it does not harm him."
"5386","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Bakr informed me from one of Banu Sa'd that he heard him claim he heard Ibn Abi Waqqas saying: He used to pray the dawn prayer on Friday in Madinah, then ride to his palace in al-Aqiq and not observe Jumu'ah, and between that is less than a barid or about that."
"5387","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that it used to be said: When a person dozes on Friday, let him leave that seat and sit in another seat, or strike his head three times, for that is from Satan. Then he gestured, closing his fist then striking with the fingertips, with the other fist with clenched nails gathered."
"5388","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: Malik ibn Abi Sahm informed me that he dozed while the imam was delivering the sermon, and either Ibn Umar gestured to him or motioned to him to leave that spot and move back from it."
"5389","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that it used to be said: When a man dozes during al-Jumu'ah while the imam is delivering the sermon, that is the seat of Satan, so let him leave it."
"5390","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: When a man dozes on Friday while the imam is delivering the sermon, he is commanded to stand and sit in other than his seat."
"5391","Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to say: When a person dozes on Friday, he leaves his seat; as for stepping over, no, but let him shift and let those around him wake him. And Abd al-Razzaq adopts this."
"5392","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar used to throw pebbles at those who slept while the imam was delivering the sermon."
"5393","Ibn Jurayj said: And it reached me from Ibn Sirin that he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a person dozes on Friday, let him move from that seat of his."
"5394","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I saw Ibn al-Musayyib sitting with his knees drawn up on Friday beside the maqsurah while the imam was delivering the sermon."
"5395","From Hisham ibn Hassan, that he saw al-Hasan sitting with his knees drawn up on Friday while the imam was delivering the sermon."
"5396","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I saw Ata sitting with his knees drawn up while the imam was delivering the sermon on Friday."
"5397","From al-Thawri, from Tawbah, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, that he used to sit with his knees drawn up on Friday, facing the imam, not turning right or left."
"5398","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man to sit with his knees drawn up on Friday while the imam is delivering the sermon."
"5399","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: There is no day greater with Allah than Friday; in it Allah decreed the creation of the heavens and the earth, and in it the Hour will come, and the sun does not rise on Friday except that the land and sea, stones and trees, and whatever Allah created of things except the two weighty beings fear, and in it is an hour which no Muslim meets asking Allah for something except He gives it to him. Ma'mar said: And I heard Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil narrating similar to this, I only know that he raised it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5400","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus narrated to me from his father that he used to transmit a narration from Ka'b, or part of it: Allah did not create a day greater than Friday; in it the creation of the heavens and earth was decreed, and in it the Hour will come, and the sun does not rise on a Friday except that the land, sea, stones, and whatever Allah created of things except the two weighty beings dread its rising, and in Friday there is an hour in which no Muslim servant asks Allah for something except He gives it to him."
"5401","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Abu Hurayrah and Ka'b met, and Abu Hurayrah said: In Friday there is an hour which no Muslim man meets asking Allah the Exalted therein for good except He gives it to him."
"5402","Ka'b said: Shall I not narrate to you about Friday? Ka'b said: When it is Friday, the heavens and the earth, the land and the sea, the trees and the soil, the water and the creatures, all dread except the son of Adam and Satan. He said: And the angels surround the doors of the mosque, writing who came first, then when the imam comes out they fold their scrolls; whoever comes after that comes for the due of Allah and what is decreed upon him. And it is due upon every pubescent man to wash therein as his washing from janabah. And the sun never rose nor set on a day greater than Friday, and charity therein is greater than on other days. Ibn Abbas said: This is the narration of Abu Hurayrah and Ka'b, and I hold that if one has his family's perfume he should touch some of it that day."
"5403","From Ma'mar, from one who heard Anas ibn Malik saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The days were shown to me, and I saw Friday, and its splendor and light pleased me, and I saw in it as it were a black speck, so I said: What is this? It was said: In it the Hour will come."
"5404","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The days were shown to me, and Friday was shown to me in a mirror, or he said: like a mirror, and I saw in it a black speck, so I said: What is this? It was said: In it the Hour will come."
"5405","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Salman: Do you know what Friday is? In it your father Adam was gathered, i.e. his clay was gathered."
"5406","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Al-Agharr Abu Abdullah, the companion of Abu Hurayrah, informed me from Abu Hurayrah, he said: When it is Friday, the angels sit at the doors of the mosque writing who comes to al-Jumu'ah, then when the imam comes out the angels fold the scrolls and enter to listen to the remembrance."
"5407","He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who comes early to al-Jumu'ah is like one who gifts a camel, then like one who gifts a cow, then like one who gifts a sheep, then like one who gifts a chicken, then like one who gifts - I think he said - an egg."
"5408","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'qub informed me from Abu Abdullah ibn Ishaq that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The sun neither rises nor sets on a day better than Friday, and there is no beast except it dreads Friday except these two weighty beings, the jinn and men. Upon every door of the mosque are two angels writing the first, then the first, like a man who offered a cow, and like a man who offered a sheep, and like a man who offered a bird, and like a man who offered an egg; then when the imam sits, the scrolls are folded."
"5409","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from Tawus, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When it is Friday, the angels sit at the doors of the mosque and write the people according to their coming, then when the imam sits, the scrolls are folded and the merits cease; whoever comes then comes only for the due of the prayer, and their merit is like the merit of the owner of the camel over the owner of the cow and over the owner of the sheep."
"5410","Ibn Jurayj said: And al-Walid informed me, he said, and it used to be said: When it is Friday, the angels sit at the doors of the mosque writing the people according to their stations; whoever comes before the imam sits, they write: so-and-so is of the forerunners, and so-and-so is of the forerunners; then when the imam sits upon the minbar, they fold their scrolls and sit with the people; whoever comes after the imam sits upon the minbar is written: so-and-so witnessed the sermon; whoever comes after the prayer is established is written: so-and-so witnessed al-Jumu'ah. So thus are their stations, from the camel down to the gnat. And sometimes the man who used to come early to al-Jumu'ah is absent, so the angels say: What kept so-and-so away, and that grieves them, so they say: Come, let us supplicate for him, and they say: O Allah, if error has detained so-and-so then guide him, or poverty then enrich him, or illness then heal him."
"5411","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sumayy, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When it is Friday, and one of you washes as he washes from janabah, then goes early in the first hour, he has of reward like a camel, and the first of the hour and its last are alike; then the second hour is like a bull, its first and last alike; then the third is like a horned ram, its first and last alike; then the fourth hour is like a chicken, its first and last alike; then like an egg. Then when the imam sits, the scrolls are folded and the angels come to listen to the remembrance; then he is forgiven when he listens and is silent, what is between the two Jumu'ahs plus three days more."
"5412","From Ibn Jurayj, from Umar ibn Muhammad, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asadi, from Aws ibn Aws, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When it is Friday, one of you washes his head, then washes, then goes early and arrives early, then draws near, listens and is silent, he has for every step he takes like a year's fasting and standing."
"5413","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Al-Ala informed me from Ibn Darah, the freedman of Uthman, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Hour will not come on Saturday, nor on Sunday, nor on Monday, nor on Tuesday, nor on Wednesday, nor on Thursday, then he fell silent."
"5414","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, he said: I heard Ubayd ibn Umayr saying: Friday - the Resurrection will come on it."
"5415","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ubayd ibn Umayr: The best day on which the sun rose is Friday; in it Adam was created, and in it the Hour will come. And when Allah created Adam, He breathed the spirit into him and it moved in him, then He breathed into him again and he sat upright, then he sneezed, and Allah cast upon his tongue: All praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds, so the angels said: May Allah have mercy on you."
"5416","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani, from Aws ibn Aws, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever washes and washes fully, and goes early and arrives early, and draws near the imam and listens attentively, for every step he takes is a year's fasting and standing, and that is easy for Allah."
"5417","From Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In Friday there is an hour which no Muslim meets while praying, asking Allah therein for something, except He gives it to him."
"5418","From Ma'mar, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying while upon the minbar: In Friday there is an hour, and he gestured with his palm as though minimizing it; no Muslim servant meets it asking Allah therein for something except He gives it to him. Then he gestured to us how the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured, so he joined his fingers together and bent them somewhat, then closed them, and did not spread them."
"5419","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: In al-Jumu'ah there is an hour in which no Muslim asks Allah for something while praying except He gives it to him. He said: And Abu Hurayrah would gesture with his hand minimizing it. Ata said: Also from some of the people of knowledge: it is after al-Asr. It was said to him: But there is no prayer after al-Asr. He said: No, but whoever remains in his prayer-place, not leaving it, is in prayer."
"5420","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to seek the hour in which supplication is answered on Friday after al-Asr. Ibn Tawus said: And my father died in an hour he loved; he died on Friday after al-Asr."
"5421","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about the hour in which supplication is answered on Friday, and he said: I have not heard anything about it to narrate except that Ka'b used to say: If a man divided his Friday among Fridays, he would hit that hour."
"5422","From Ma'mar, he said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me, saying: There was a man seeking the hour in which supplication is answered on Friday, then he dozed a doze on Friday and was visited in sleep and told: Wake up, for this is the hour you were seeking, and that was at the sun's decline. And al-Hasan thereafter used to seek it at the sun's decline."
"5423","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The hour that comes on Friday is between al-Asr until the sun sets."
"5424","From Umar ibn Dharr, from Yahya ibn Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in the al-Asr prayer on Friday, the people behind him, when a dog appeared passing before them, so the dog fell and died before passing. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to the people, he said: Which of you supplicated against this dog? A man said: I supplicated against it, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You supplicated against it in an hour in which supplication is answered."
"5425","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah narrated to me that he heard Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf saying: I heard Abdullah ibn Salam saying: The day is twelve hours, and the hour mentioned of Friday is the last hours of the day."
"5426","He said: And Musa also narrated to me, he said: A man said to a man: How do they claim it is it, while one does not pray therein? The other said: Abu Hurayrah used to say: One remains in prayer so long as he does not leave his prayer-place or speak."
"5427","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim narrated to me, I only know it from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas. Ibn Jurayj said: And Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to me likewise from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, and he was asked about that hour, and he said: Allah created Adam after al-Asr on Friday, and created him from the surface of the whole earth, its red and its black, its good and its bad, and therefore among his children are the black and the red, the good and the bad. Then He made His angels prostrate to him and settled him in His Garden, but before that day ended he disobeyed Him, so He expelled him from it."
"5428","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim narrated to me from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: I said to Ibn Abbas: O father of Abbas, the hour mentioned on Friday? He said: Allah knows best, several times. Allah created Adam in the last hours of Friday, creating him from the surface of the whole earth, its red and its black, its good and its bad, its rugged and its plain; therefore among his children are the good and the bad, the red and the black, the plain and the rugged. Then He breathed into him of His spirit, settled him in His Garden, commanded the angels and they prostrated to him, and took a covenant from him, but he forgot, so he was called man. Before the sun set that day, He expelled him from it."
"5429","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Isma'il ibn Kathir informed me that Tawus informed him that the hour of Friday in which the Hour will come, in which Allah sent Adam down, and in which no Muslim supplicates Allah with a righteous supplication except he is answered, is from when the sun yellows until it sets."
"5430","He said: And he narrated to me from al-A'raj, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Abu Hurayrah set out to Sham, and he and Ka'b met; Abu Hurayrah would narrate from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Ka'b would narrate from the Torah, until they came to the hour that is on Friday. Abu Hurayrah said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: On Friday there is an hour in which no Muslim servant asks Allah for something except He gives it to him. Ka'b said: But on one Friday of the year. Abu Hurayrah said: No. Ka'b said: Ha, Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth - on every Friday. Then Abu Hurayrah came to Madinah, and he and Abdullah ibn Salam met, and Abu Hurayrah mentioned to him what Ka'b said about Friday, and Abdullah said: He lied. Abu Hurayrah said: But he retracted."
"5431","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Al-Abbas narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Maslamah al-Ansari, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In al-Jumu'ah there is an hour which no Muslim servant meets asking Allah therein for good except He gives it to him, and it is after al-Asr."
"5432","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Salam, that he said: I know that hour. I said to him: O my brother, I am not a man to begrudge it to him; tell me it. He said: It is the last hour of Friday before the sun sets. I said: Did you not say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No Muslim servant meets it while in prayer - and that hour is not a prayer time. He said: Did you not hear the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever prays then sits waiting for the prayer remains in his prayer until the next prayer that follows comes to him. He said: And in it Adam was created, and in it he was sent down from the Garden, and in it he was forgiven, and in it he died, and in it the Hour will come."
"5433","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Asim informed me from Abdullah ibn Yuhannis, from Salih, the freedman of Mu'awiyah, he said: I said to Abu Hurayrah: They claim Laylat al-Qadr has been lifted. He said: He who said that lied. I said: Is it in every Ramadan I face? He said: Yes. He said: I said: Do they claim the hour on Friday in which no Muslim supplicates except he is answered has been lifted? He said: He who said so lied. I said: Is it in every Jumu'ah I face? He said: Yes."
"5434","He said: Yahya ibn Rabi'ah reported to us, he said: I heard Ata saying: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: On Friday there is an hour which no servant meets praying or waiting for the prayer, supplicating Allah therein for something, except He answers him."
"5435","From Ma'mar, from one who heard Anas saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: From al-Jumu'ah to al-Jumu'ah is expiation, and the five prayers are expiations for what is between them so long as major sins are avoided. He said: A man said: O Prophet of Allah, does al-Jumu'ah to al-Jumu'ah expiate? He said: Yes, plus three days more."
"5436","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Ajlan, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Abdullah ibn Wadi'ah ibn al-Hirzi, from Abu Dharr, he said: And I heard Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abi Dhi'b from Abu Dharr, he said: Whoever washes on Friday and washes well, wears of his good clothes, touches what Allah decreed for him of his family's perfume or oil, then goes to al-Jumu'ah and does not separate two, he is forgiven what is between the two Jumu'ahs plus three days more."
"5437","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever uses the siwak on Friday, then washes as he washes from janabah, then touches perfume, then wears his two garments, then goes early to the mosque and does not separate two, nor speaks until the imam stands, he is forgiven what is between the two Jumu'ahs."
"5438","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, that Jabir ibn Abdullah said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you should make his brother stand on Friday and take his seat, but let him say: Make room."
"5439","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Nafi' saying: Ibn Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you should make his brother stand from his seat then take his place. I said to him: Is it on Friday? He said: On Friday and other than it. Nafi' said: When a man would stand for Ibn Umar from his seat, he would not sit in it."
"5440","He said: Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Salim, that Ibn Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you should make his brother stand and sit in his place. A man would stand for Ibn Umar from his seat and he would not sit in his seat. From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likewise, he said: But let him say: Make room and spread out."
"5441","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Ibn Shihab, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dies on Friday night or Friday day is cleared of the trial of the grave - or he said - protected from the trial of the grave, and written a martyr."
"5442","From Ibn Jurayj, from Rabi'ah ibn Sayf, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Cleared of the trial of the grave. From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah ibn Hantab, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likewise."
"5443","Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-A'rabi reported to us, he said: Abu Ya'qub Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Abbad al-Dabari narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about the prayer before the imam comes out on Fitr day. He said: When the sun has risen, then pray. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim informed me that Mujahid used to pray between them."
"5444","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Anas, Abu Hurayrah, al-Hasan and his brother Sa'id, and Jabir ibn Zayd used to pray before the imam comes out and after him."
"5445","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: I saw Anas ibn Malik and al-Hasan praying before the Eid prayer."
